SUMMIT_MCQS = [
    # G20 South Africa 2025 (21001-21008)
    {"id":21001,"question":"The G20 Leaders' Summit 2025 was held in which city, marking the first-ever G20 hosted on the African continent?","option_a":"Cape Town, South Africa","option_b":"Nairobi, Kenya","option_c":"Johannesburg, South Africa","option_d":"Lagos, Nigeria","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The 20th G20 Summit was held at the Johannesburg Expo Centre, South Africa on November 22-23, 2025 — first G20 ever hosted on the African continent, signalling a historic shift in global economic governance toward the Global South. South Africa's G20 presidency (under President Cyril Ramaphosa) prioritized African development, debt sustainability, and UN Security Council reform — pushing back against Western dominance in multilateral institutions. India played a bridging role in supporting consensus on climate finance and development priorities. South Africa's presidency followed Brazil (2024) and handed over to the USA (2026), marking a reversal in Global South leadership. The Johannesburg Declaration adopted a 122-point agenda emphasizing Ubuntu philosophy (African values of interconnectedness), signalling Africa's centrality to 21st-century development discourse.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21002,"question":"What was the official theme of South Africa's G20 Presidency and Johannesburg Summit 2025?","option_a":"Building a Resilient World Together","option_b":"Solidarity, Equality, Sustainability","option_c":"Collaboration for the Intelligent Age","option_d":"A Spirit of Dialogue","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"'Solidarity, Equality, Sustainability' was South Africa's G20 2025 theme, rooted in the African Ubuntu philosophy meaning 'I am because we are.' The 122-point Johannesburg Declaration operationalized this tri-pillar vision: Solidarity=multilateral cooperation free from hegemony, Equality=fair economic opportunities for the Global South with debt relief, Sustainability=long-term development without sacrificing climate commitments. The theme directly challenged Western consensus-building and elevated African/Global South priorities in climate finance, technology transfer, and UN reform. India endorsed this pro-South positioning, aligning with its own G20 2023 focus on inclusive development. The declaration's unprecedented adoption at the summit's OPENING (not closing) signalled South Africa's determination to proceed with multilateral outcomes regardless of US absence.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21003,"question":"Which major G20 member was ABSENT from the G20 Johannesburg Summit 2025?","option_a":"China","option_b":"Russia","option_c":"United States","option_d":"United Kingdom","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The USA did not participate in the G20 Johannesburg Summit 2025 — a symbolic statement reflecting Trump's transactional foreign policy and diminished multilateral engagement. President Trump's absence, coupled with Secretary Blinken's limited representation, signalled a withdrawal from Global South consensus-building. This prompted South Africa to adopt the 122-point declaration at the summit's opening regardless of US participation, demonstrating that multilateral outcomes are achievable without hegemonic consensus. Argentina was the only nation PRESENT that did not subscribe to the final declaration — FM Quirno cited missing 'geopolitical context' on the ongoing Iran-Israel-US war (2026), highlighting Global North-South divides on Middle East diplomacy. India capitalized on this moment to reinforce South-South cooperation and assert Indian diplomatic leadership within BRICS and the developing world coalition.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21004,"question":"The Johannesburg G20 2025 Declaration referenced 'Ubuntu' philosophy. Ubuntu is a concept from which tradition?","option_a":"East Asian Confucian thought","option_b":"South American indigenous tradition","option_c":"African philosophy emphasising interconnectedness and shared humanity","option_d":"South Asian Vedantic philosophy","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Ubuntu is a Bantu philosophical concept meaning 'I am because we are' — emphasising interconnectedness, collective responsibility, and shared humanity over individualism. South Africa embedded this into the G20 Johannesburg 2025 Declaration as a deliberate counter-narrative to Western liberal individualism and neoliberal economics. Ubuntu became the foundational principle for the theme 'Solidarity, Equality, Sustainability,' positioning African wisdom traditions as legitimate frameworks for global governance. The choice signalled South Africa's assertion of African intellectual authority and rejected Western hegemony in defining development models. India resonated with Ubuntu's parallels to concepts like 'Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam' (the world is one family), enabling India-South Africa coordination on Global South interests.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21005,"question":"The G20 Johannesburg 2025 Declaration included a call for reform of which major international body?","option_a":"IMF","option_b":"World Bank","option_c":"UN Security Council","option_d":"WTO","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The 122-point G20 Johannesburg Declaration called for UN Security Council (UNSC) reform — a long-standing demand by Global South nations (India, Brazil, South Africa) who seek permanent seats reflecting 21st-century geopolitical realities. The UNSC call reflected frustration with Cold War-era structures perpetuating Western dominance. India strongly backed UNSC reform alongside African representation, positioning itself as a voice for systemic global governance restructuring. Other landmark outcomes included: G20 Critical Minerals Framework (voluntary supply-chain tracking), 'Mission 300' (electrify 300 million Africans by 2030), AI for Africa initiative, universal early warning systems for climate/disaster by 2027, debt sustainability mechanisms for developing nations, and a Debt Sustainability Framework addressing the debt crisis facing small island states and LDCs.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21006,"question":"Which country will host the G20 Summit in 2026?","option_a":"Germany","option_b":"Japan","option_c":"United States","option_d":"India","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"USA will host the G20 in Miami in 2026 — a transition marking return of Western dominance in G20 leadership after Global South chairs (Brazil 2024, South Africa 2025). The chairmanship was formally handed over from South Africa to the USA on January 12, 2026 (a low-key, offsite ceremony reflecting US absence from Johannesburg). India's G20 presidency (2023) had advanced the African Union's permanent membership and Development Agenda priorities; USA's 2026 presidency is expected to emphasize economic competition, digital economy regulation, and Indo-Pacific security architecture — competing with China's influence in BRICS and regional forums. Host sequence: Brazil (2024) → South Africa (2025) → USA/Miami (2026).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21007,"question":"At the G20 Johannesburg 2025, what voluntary framework was endorsed for managing critical mineral supply chains?","option_a":"G20 Strategic Minerals Accord","option_b":"G20 Critical Minerals Framework","option_c":"Global Mineral Governance Protocol","option_d":"Johannesburg Raw Materials Declaration","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"The G20 Critical Minerals Framework (voluntary, non-binding) was endorsed at Johannesburg 2025 — establishing governance principles for tracking supply chains, labour costs, environmental damages, and community impacts in mining. Framework recognizes minerals (lithium, cobalt, rare earths) critical for green energy transition are concentrated in few countries, creating geopolitical vulnerabilities. India, as both a consumer (EV manufacturing ramp-up) and producer (rare earths), supported principles emphasizing developing-nation access to technology and fair pricing. The framework is deliberately voluntary to secure buy-in from major mining nations (Australia, Indonesia, Peru) and avoid binding commitments affecting sovereignty. This followed parallel work at G7 Kananaskis 2025, showing coordination between competing forums on supply-chain risk.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21008,"question":"Which nation opposed the G20 Johannesburg 2025 final declaration, citing missing geopolitical context on the Middle East?","option_a":"Russia","option_b":"Argentina","option_c":"USA","option_d":"Hungary","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Argentina was the only G20 nation PRESENT that did not subscribe to the Johannesburg 2025 Declaration — a symbolic dissent reflecting Argentina's pro-US alignment under President Javier Milei (elected 2023). Argentine FM Julio Quirno stated 'context and geopolitical facts' were critically missing regarding the Middle East (the escalating Iran-Israel-US war, February 28-April 8, 2026). Argentina's refusal to sign signalled divergence from Global South consensus and alignment with Western positions on Middle East intervention. The USA was entirely absent from the summit, making Argentina's dissent the sole internal opposition. India and other G20 members supported the declaration despite geopolitical complexity, endorsing South Africa's framing of Ubuntu-inspired multilateralism as superior to bifurcated Cold War logics.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # G7 Kananaskis Canada 2025 (21009-21014)
    {"id":21009,"question":"The G7 Summit 2025 was hosted by which country, and who was the host leader?","option_a":"Italy, PM Giorgia Meloni","option_b":"USA, President Donald Trump","option_c":"Canada, PM Mark Carney","option_d":"Japan, PM Sanae Takaichi","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Canada hosted the 51st G7 Summit at Kananaskis, Alberta in the Rocky Mountains (June 15-17, 2025). PM Mark Carney (newly elected Feb 2025) hosted in a remote location partly for security. Trump's early departure (~12 hours) citing the escalating Israel-Iran Twelve-Day War (June 2025) disrupted cohesion, reflecting Trump's transactional foreign policy and skepticism of multilateral commitment. The remaining six G7 members (EU, UK, France, Germany, Italy, Japan, Canada) issued a separate statement on AI and critical minerals without the US, signalling that G7 cohesion was fragmenting. No formal joint communiqué was signed after Trump's exit, weakening G7 political authority. This recalled Trump's 2018 Charlevoix early departure and foreshadowed broader unilateral US posturing in 2026.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21010,"question":"At which specific location in Canada was the G7 Summit 2025 held?","option_a":"Banff, Alberta","option_b":"Vancouver, British Columbia","option_c":"Quebec City, Quebec","option_d":"Kananaskis, Alberta","correct_answer":"D","explanation":"G7 Summit 2025 was held in Kananaskis, Alberta — a remote mountain resort in the Canadian Rockies chosen for security and symbolic separation from public/media scrutiny. The isolation also insulated against anti-globalization protests. After Trump's abrupt early departure, the remaining six G7 leaders (France, Germany, Italy, Japan, UK, Canada, EU) issued a joint statement without the US on AI governance, quantum computing, critical minerals, wildfire cooperation, and migrant smuggling networks — signalling that G7 could function without US consensus. This demonstrated the fragility of Western multilateral institutions under Trump's transactional approach and elevated alternatives (EU-Japan-India coordination on Indo-Pacific security) as substitute frameworks.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21011,"question":"US President Trump departed the G7 Kananaskis Summit 2025 early, citing which international crisis?","option_a":"North Korea missile launches","option_b":"Taiwan Strait tensions","option_c":"Israel-Iran conflict","option_d":"Russia-Ukraine battlefield escalation","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Trump left G7 Kananaskis early (after ~12 hours on June 15, 2025) citing the Israel-Iran conflict — specifically the escalating June 2025 Twelve-Day War (June 2-13) involving sustained Israeli strikes on Iranian military infrastructure and Iranian retaliation. Trump's framing centered on US interests in Middle East military dominance rather than G7 consensus-building, consistent with his 'America First' doctrine. His departure recalled his 2018 Charlevoix G7 early exit and signalled that Trump prioritizes bilateral US interests over multilateral institutions. The remaining six G7 leaders (Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan, EU) issued a joint statement without the US, demonstrating resilience of G7 minus-one diplomacy and concern that the Western alliance was fragmenting under American unilateralism.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21012,"question":"What tariff threat did Trump make toward Canada after the G7 Kananaskis Summit 2025?","option_a":"10% tariff on all Canadian goods","option_b":"25% tariff on Canadian steel","option_c":"35% tariff on Canadian goods if no trade deal reached","option_d":"50% tariff on Canadian energy exports","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Trump threatened 35% tariffs on Canadian goods (effective August 1, 2025) if Canada did not reach a swift trade deal renegotiating USMCA. Trump's aggressive tariff coercion against G7 allies (especially Canada and UK) was a defining tension of Kananaskis, undermining Western trade cohesion. His threats signalled a protectionist turn contradicting traditional G7 trade liberalism and creating economic fragmentation. India viewed this as validation of its own strategic autonomy and reduced reliance on Western markets, accelerating India-ASEAN, India-EU FTA, and BRICS de-dollarization initiatives. G7 2026 will be hosted by France in Évian-les-Bains (June 15-17), where trade tensions are expected to dominate discussions.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21013,"question":"The G7 Kananaskis 2025 remaining six leaders' joint statement reached consensus on which topics?","option_a":"Quantum internet standards","option_b":"Space exploration cooperation","option_c":"AI, quantum computing, and critical minerals","option_d":"5G network security","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The six remaining G7 leaders (without Trump/USA) reached consensus on AI, quantum computing, and critical minerals supply-chain security. The statement emphasized AI should 'grow prosperity, benefit societies, and address global challenges' — signalling a pro-innovation stance balancing regulation with economic growth. This marked a shift from prior G7 AI governance focus (COP29, COP30) which prioritized existential risks. The joint statement demonstrated that G7 minus the US could still project technical authority and coordinate on technology standards. India noted this as validation of plurilateral formats (QUAD, I2U2, ALBA) as alternatives to G7/G20 consensus when Western unity fractured. Critical minerals language aligned with India's interests in ensuring developing-nation access to clean-tech supply chains.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21014,"question":"Which country will host the G7 Summit in 2026?","option_a":"USA","option_b":"France","option_c":"UK","option_d":"Germany","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"France will host the G7 Summit in 2026 at Évian-les-Bains (June 15-17, 2026) — second time France hosted at Évian (previous summit 2003). President Emmanuel Macron, re-elected 2022, will lead as France asserts European autonomy from Trump's unilateralism. Évian 2026 is expected to address transatlantic tensions, European defense spending, NATO cohesion, and China-Russia containment. Host sequence: Italy Puglia (2024) → Canada Kananaskis (2025) → France Évian (2026). G7 members: USA, UK, Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan + EU. India views France's hosting as an opportunity for stronger India-EU FTA implementation (signed Jan 27, 2026) and coordination on Indo-Pacific security independent of US leadership.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # NATO The Hague 2025 (21015-21020)
    {"id":21015,"question":"The NATO Summit 2025 was held in which city on June 24-25, 2025?","option_a":"Brussels, Belgium","option_b":"Warsaw, Poland","option_c":"The Hague, Netherlands","option_d":"Vilnius, Lithuania","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"NATO Summit 2025 was held at the World Forum, The Hague, Netherlands on June 24-25, 2025 — months after Trump's return to power raised questions about NATO's Article 5 commitment. Secretary-General Mark Rutte (Netherlands, ex-PM) presided over the largest NATO expenditure increase since Cold War. Key outcomes: 5% GDP defense spending target (all members except Spain), €40 billion Ukraine aid package, Article 5 commitment reaffirmed despite Trump's earlier skepticism, NATO Strategy updated. India observed NATO expansion as geopolitically significant for Indo-Pacific security (Australia, Japan, South Korea coordination through NATO). NATO 2026 will be in Ankara, Turkey (July 7-8), further involving NATO in broader Indo-Pacific deterrence against China-Russia alignment. NATO now has 32 members (Sweden joined March 2024).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21016,"question":"The most significant outcome of NATO Hague Summit 2025 was a new defense spending target. What was it?","option_a":"2% of GDP","option_b":"3% of GDP","option_c":"4% of GDP","option_d":"5% of GDP","correct_answer":"D","explanation":"NATO Hague 2025 adopted 5% GDP defense spending — a seismic jump from the 2014 Wales Summit benchmark of 2%, reflecting escalating Russian threats post-2022 Ukraine invasion and new China-Russia-Iran axis dynamics. Breakdown: 3.5% core military capabilities + 1.5% defense-related (cyber, infrastructure, space, nuclear). All 32 NATO members agreed except Spain (PM Sánchez resisted). This represents NATO's largest peacetime rearmament, with combined annual defense budgets approaching $2 trillion. India noted this militarization of Europe creates vulnerabilities for India's defense exports and arms-control advocacy; simultaneously, it validates India's own defense modernization against Pakistan-China nexus. The 5% target signalled Western resolve to deter Russian expansion and contain Chinese military growth in hybrid domains.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21017,"question":"Under NATO's 5% GDP defense spending target (The Hague 2025), how is the spending broken down?","option_a":"4% military + 1% infrastructure","option_b":"3.5% core military + 1.5% defense-related (cyber/infrastructure)","option_c":"2.5% military + 2.5% security","option_d":"All 5% directed to direct military capabilities","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"NATO's 5% GDP target (Hague 2025): 3.5% for core military capabilities (hardware, personnel, readiness) + 1.5% for broader defense-related sectors (cyber defense, space operations, infrastructure, intelligence, nuclear deterrence). This split reflects modern hybrid warfare where kinetic and non-kinetic domains are equally critical. The structure allows NATO members flexibility in allocating budgets while maintaining credible conventional + cyber + nuclear deterrence. India, as a non-NATO developing democracy, views the NATO budget increase as validation of defense spending increases needed for its Pakistan/China deterrence while simultaneously pressuring Indian defense budgets upward. The framework replaces the 2014 Wales Summit target of 2% GDP, representing a 150% increase in spending intensity.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21018,"question":"Which NATO member was the sole dissenter to the 5% defense spending target at the Hague Summit 2025?","option_a":"France","option_b":"Germany","option_c":"Italy","option_d":"Spain","correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Spain was the only NATO member that did not agree to the 5% GDP defense spending target at The Hague Summit 2025. PM Pedro Sánchez's Socialist government historically resisted NATO defense escalation, citing domestic social spending priorities and pacifist constituencies. Spain's dissent reflected broader European tensions between welfare expansion and defense militarization. This isolated Spain diplomatically and raised questions about NATO consensus-building when facing Russian threats. All 31 other members (including Poland, Romania, Slovakia near Russian borders) supported the 5% target. NATO now has 32 members after Sweden joined as the 32nd member on March 7, 2024 (Finland joined 2023, upgrading NATO's Russia border coverage). Spain's position was overridden by alliance supermajority, foreshadowing challenges for binding NATO decisions when member interests diverge.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21019,"question":"How much financial aid did NATO allies pledge to Ukraine at the Hague Summit 2025?","option_a":"€20 billion","option_b":"€40 billion","option_c":"€60 billion","option_d":"€100 billion","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"NATO allies pledged €40 billion in defense aid to Ukraine for 2025 at the Hague Summit (June 24-25, 2025). The commitment represented escalating Western military support as Russia-Ukraine war intensified into its 4th year. Notably, the declaration did NOT include a Ukraine NATO membership invitation, formal timeline, or clear pathway — disappointing President Zelenskyy's hopes for stronger political commitments. NATO leadership wanted to avoid direct confrontation with Russia while maintaining Ukraine's morale. Instead, NATO emphasized enhanced military capacity-building and intelligence sharing. India maintained strict neutrality on Ukraine aid questions, criticizing both sides' geopolitical adventurism while opposing unilateral economic sanctions. The €40 billion demonstrated NATO's military commitment while avoiding irreversible escalation (no-fly zone, NATO troops on ground).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21020,"question":"Where will the NATO Summit 2026 be held?","option_a":"Warsaw, Poland","option_b":"London, United Kingdom","option_c":"Ankara, Turkey","option_d":"Oslo, Norway","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"NATO Summit 2026 will be held July 7-8, 2026 at the Beştepe Presidential Complex in Ankara, Turkey — a symbolically significant location highlighting Turkey's geopolitical centrality to NATO (controlling Bosphorus Strait, managing Syria border, balancing Russia-West relations). President Erdoğan's hosting will emphasize Turkish mediation role and NATO's southeastern flank against Russian/Iranian influence. NATO Secretary-General since October 2024: Mark Rutte (Netherlands, ex-PM), who replaced Jens Stoltenberg (10 years). India views Turkey's role as pivotal for Indo-Pacific NATO expansion (Australia, Japan, South Korea partnerships) and Istanbul as a strategic hub for Asia-Europe connectivity. Turkey's NATO membership also complicates NATO-India-QUAD coordination on China containment. Host sequence: Washington DC (2024) → The Hague (2025) → Ankara (2026).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # BRICS Brazil 2025 (21021-21028)
    {"id":21021,"question":"The 17th BRICS Summit 2025 was hosted by which country, and where?","option_a":"South Africa, Johannesburg","option_b":"Russia, Moscow","option_c":"Brazil, Rio de Janeiro","option_d":"China, Beijing","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The 17th BRICS Summit 2025 was held in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil on July 6-7, 2025 — Brazil's BRICS Chairmanship year and the first BRICS summit to feature Indonesia (11th full member, joined Jan 6, 2025) and 10 partner countries admitted at Rio. Brazil's President Lula positioned BRICS as a counterweight to Western-dominated IMF/World Bank through new BRICS Development Bank initiatives and de-dollarization proposals. This was a watershed moment: BRICS now represents 37% of global GDP (PPP) and 45% of world population, with expansion into Southeast Asia (Indonesia, Malaysia partner), Africa, and Central Asia signalling a deliberate Global South realignment. India hosted the 18th BRICS Summit in New Delhi (Sept 12-13, 2026) as BRICS Chair 2026, deepening India's leadership in non-aligned Global South diplomacy and asserting India as BRICS's Asia-Pacific anchor.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21022,"question":"Which country joined BRICS as a full member on January 6, 2025, becoming the first Southeast Asian nation in the bloc?","option_a":"Malaysia","option_b":"Vietnam","option_c":"Thailand","option_d":"Indonesia","correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Indonesia joined BRICS as a full member on January 6, 2025 — first Southeast Asian state in BRICS and the 11th full member, breaking BRICS's previous concentration in Africa, Russia, and Asia. Indonesia's bid was approved at BRICS Kazan 2024 summit but deferred entry until after Indonesia's October 2024 presidential election (Prabowo Subianto elected). Indonesia's accession was geopolitically momentous: a 270+ million population Southeast Asian democracy joining a bloc historically dominated by authoritarian regimes, signalling BRICS's broadening legitimacy and reach. India strongly supported Indonesia's entry as it balances China's influence within BRICS. Malaysia, Vietnam, Thailand remain partner countries (not full members). BRICS full members now: Brazil, Russia, India, China, South Africa, Egypt, Ethiopia, Iran, UAE, Saudi Arabia, Indonesia.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21023,"question":"At BRICS Rio Summit 2025, how many new countries were admitted as BRICS 'partner countries'?","option_a":"5","option_b":"7","option_c":"10","option_d":"13","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"10 new BRICS partner countries admitted at Rio 2025: Belarus, Bolivia, Kazakhstan, Cuba, Malaysia, Nigeria, Thailand, Uganda, Uzbekistan, and Vietnam. These create a two-tier BRICS structure: 11 full members + 10 partner nations = 21 total members representing alternative blocs (SCO members like Kazakhstan/Uzbekistan, Latin American solidarity with Cuba/Bolivia, African growth markets). Partner status provides access to BRICS development finance, trade cooperation, and digital currency initiatives without full voting rights. India supported expansion as it dilutes China's influence by bringing in Indonesia, Kazakhstan, Uzbekistan (India partners on Central Asia), and Malaysia/Vietnam (India's Act East allies). The 21-member formation positions BRICS as genuinely Global South representing non-aligned nations and creating institutional alternatives to US-led multilateralism.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21024,"question":"After Indonesia joined in 2025, how many FULL MEMBERS does BRICS have?","option_a":"10","option_b":"11","option_c":"15","option_d":"21","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"BRICS has 11 full members: Brazil, Russia, India, China, South Africa (original 5, founded 2011) + Egypt, Ethiopia, Iran, UAE, Saudi Arabia (joined January 1, 2024, BRICS+ expansion announced Kazan 2024) + Indonesia (January 6, 2025). The 10 partner countries at Rio 2025 (Belarus, Bolivia, Kazakhstan, Cuba, Malaysia, Nigeria, Thailand, Uganda, Uzbekistan, Vietnam) are NOT full members and lack voting rights in BRICS decisions. Total BRICS structure: 11 full members + 10 partners = 21 nations. India plays a crucial balancing role within BRICS, mediating between China-Russia strategic alignment and the broader development/non-aligned agenda. India's 2026 BRICS Chairmanship will likely emphasize inclusive development and technology transfer over geopolitical confrontation.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21025,"question":"Where will the 18th BRICS Summit 2026 be held?","option_a":"Moscow, Russia","option_b":"Beijing, China","option_c":"New Delhi, India","option_d":"Dubai, UAE","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"18th BRICS Summit 2026 is in New Delhi, India on September 12-13, 2026 — India's BRICS Chairmanship year. This is India's first BRICS leaders' summit since the 2024-2025 expansion to 11 full members and 10 partners, positioning India to shape BRICS's post-expansion agenda. India's presidency will likely emphasize: South-South cooperation, technology transfer to developing nations, climate justice and loss-and-damage financing (aligned with India's COP presidency advocacy), counterterrorism cooperation (aligned with SCO Tianjin condemnation of Pahalgam attack), and development finance through BRICS Development Bank. New Delhi summit will also showcase India's diplomatic leadership within non-aligned movements and validate India's global stature. Host sequence: Russia Kazan (2024) → Brazil Rio (2025) → India New Delhi (2026) → Egypt (2027).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21026,"question":"Which of these is NOT one of the 10 BRICS partner countries admitted at Rio de Janeiro 2025?","option_a":"Malaysia","option_b":"Vietnam","option_c":"Pakistan","option_d":"Nigeria","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Pakistan is NOT a BRICS partner country despite SCO membership and Strategic Partnership with China. The 10 BRICS partners (Rio 2025): Belarus, Bolivia, Kazakhstan, Cuba, Malaysia, Nigeria, Thailand, Uganda, Uzbekistan, Vietnam. Pakistan's exclusion from BRICS reflects India-Pakistan antagonism (India's veto power in consensus-based organizations) and Pakistan's closer alignment with China-centric blocs (SCO, China-Pakistan Economic Corridor). India's diplomatic leverage ensures Pakistan cannot enter BRICS without explicit Indian support. Pakistan's focus remains SCO, Shanghai (financial markets), and bilateral relations with China-Saudi Arabia. Notably, Malaysia and Vietnam (India's Act East partners) were admitted as BRICS partners, strengthening India's Southeast Asia engagement through multilateral forums.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21027,"question":"BRICS was originally 'BRIC'. When did South Africa join, and when was the FIRST expansion after that?","option_a":"South Africa joined 2010; next expansion 2023","option_b":"South Africa joined 2010; next expansion January 2024","option_c":"South Africa joined 2012; next expansion 2024","option_d":"South Africa joined 2015; next expansion 2024","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"South Africa joined BRIC in 2010 (making it BRICS). BRICS remained the original 5 members (Brazil, Russia, India, China, South Africa) for 14 years until the first major expansion at BRICS Kazan 2024. First expansion: January 1, 2024 when Egypt, Ethiopia, Iran, UAE, and Saudi Arabia joined (5 new members, broadening BRICS into Africa, Middle East, Gulf). Indonesia followed January 6, 2025 as the 11th member. Goldman Sachs economist Jim O'Neill coined 'BRIC' in 2001 as an investment thesis; BRICS formalized as political bloc through Shanghai Cooperation 2009. This 14-year stability then rapid 2024-2025 expansion signals BRICS's acceleration as counter-Western institutional alternative and India's crucial role in managing expansion's inclusivity/consensual expansion.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21028,"question":"BRICS full members (11 nations) collectively represent approximately what percentage of global GDP (PPP)?","option_a":"Around 20%","option_b":"Around 30%","option_c":"Around 37%","option_d":"Around 50%","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"BRICS 11 full members represent approximately 37% of global GDP (PPP) as of 2025 — exceeding G7 (30% PPP) and signalling a fundamental rebalancing of economic power from West to Global South. BRICS represents 45%+ of world population (~3.5 billion people). India contributes ~$13 trillion (PPP) to BRICS GDP, positioned second only to China within the bloc. De-dollarization is a key BRICS economic priority (Brazil Lula advanced this at Rio 2025), developing alternatives to USD for trade settlement, currency reserves, and development finance. BRICS Development Bank, BRICS reserve currency proposals, and Common Development Bank are mechanisms to reduce dependence on US-controlled financial architecture (IMF, World Bank, SWIFT). India supports BRICS financial independence while maintaining strategic partnerships with developed economies.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # SCO Tianjin 2025 (21029-21034)
    {"id":21029,"question":"The 25th SCO Heads of State Summit 2025 was held in which city?","option_a":"Beijing, China","option_b":"Shanghai, China","option_c":"Tianjin, China","option_d":"Astana, Kazakhstan","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"25th SCO Heads of State Council was held in Tianjin, China (Bohai Bay, Jinghai District) on August 31-September 1, 2025 — described as the largest SCO summit in history with unprecedented scale. Key outcomes: Tianjin Declaration affirming regional security and multilateralism, SCO Development Bank established (with initial capitalization from China), SCO 2035 Strategy extending SCO's vision beyond 2025, counter-terrorism cooperation programme aligned with India's security priorities, cultural/civilizational dialogue framework. 20 key documents signed. India participated at full leadership level, with PM Modi meeting President Xi Jinping bilaterally (first meeting on Chinese soil in 7 years), signalling India-China normalization post-Galwan tensions. SCO now encompasses 10 full members covering ~30% of world population across Eurasia.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21030,"question":"A significant bilateral at SCO Tianjin 2025: PM Modi met President Xi Jinping. What made this historically notable?","option_a":"First India-China bilateral in 10 years","option_b":"First time Modi met Xi on Chinese soil in 7 years","option_c":"First meeting after India joined SCO in 2017","option_d":"First meeting since Galwan clash 2020","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"PM Modi met Xi Jinping at SCO Tianjin 2025 (September 1, 2025) — first time Modi met Xi on Chinese soil in 7 years (since Wuhan 2018 informal summit). The bilateral signalled India-China normalization post-2020 Galwan clash, with both leaders pledging cooperation on resolving Himalayan border disputes through LAC patrolling agreements (Depsang, Demchok resolved Oct-Nov 2024). Modi outlined India's SCO vision on three pillars: Security (counterterrorism), Connectivity (infrastructure), Opportunity (economic growth). The meeting demonstrated multilateral forums (SCO) can facilitate bilateral reconciliation when direct diplomacy is strained. India's strategic objective: balance China's economic dominance within BRICS/SCO through partnerships with Central Asian nations (Kazakhstan, Tajikistan, Kyrgyzstan) and mediation between Russia-China axis and India-Japan-Australia cooperation.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21031,"question":"What major institutional decision was taken at the SCO Tianjin Summit 2025?","option_a":"SCO military alliance treaty signed","option_b":"Establishment of the SCO Development Bank","option_c":"SCO free trade agreement","option_d":"SCO permanent peacekeeping force","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"SCO Development Bank was established at Tianjin 2025 as a multilateral development financing institution to fund infrastructure, connectivity, and development projects among SCO members — modeled on Asian Development Bank but oriented toward Eurasian corridors. China pledged initial capitalization: 2 billion yuan ($280M) as grant aid + 10 billion yuan ($1.4B) in concessional loans over three years. Bank headquarters: Beijing. This represents SCO's institutional maturation beyond security cooperation into economic integration. India negotiated participation in bank governance to ensure equitable decision-making and prevent Chinese dominance. 20 key documents signed: Tianjin Declaration, SCO 2035 Strategy (vision to 2035), counter-extremism cooperation framework, cultural/civilizational dialogue platform. The bank enables BRICS/SCO members to reduce dependence on Western IMF/World Bank architecture and finance development on South-South basis.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21032,"question":"SCO Tianjin 2025 explicitly condemned which Indian security event — a rare SCO condemnation of terrorism targeting India?","option_a":"Pulwama attack 2019","option_b":"Uri attack 2016","option_c":"Pahalgam terror attack April 22, 2025","option_d":"Parliament attack 2001","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"The SCO Tianjin 2025 declaration explicitly condemned the Pahalgam terror attack (April 22, 2025, Anantnag district, Jammu & Kashmir — 26 civilians killed, mostly tourists, by Pakistan-backed terror groups) and called for bringing perpetrators to justice with SCO member cooperation. This was a significant diplomatic victory for India: China and Pakistan (both SCO full members) joined consensus condemning terrorism in Indian territory — a rare achievement given Pakistan-India antagonism within SCO. The condemnation validated India's counterterrorism priorities and positioned India as a victim of cross-border terrorism deserving multilateral support. India leveraged this to advance SCO Regional Anti-Terrorism Structure (RATS) and counterextremism initiatives. Pakistan's agreement signalled constraints on its terror proxy strategy within multilateral forums. SCO's condemnation was more explicit than typical diplomatic language, reflecting post-Galwan India-China warming and consensus-building within the organization.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21033,"question":"SCO Tianjin 2025 had 10 full members. Which of these is NOT a full SCO member?","option_a":"Iran","option_b":"Belarus","option_c":"Afghanistan","option_d":"Uzbekistan","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Afghanistan is NOT a full SCO member (holds observer status along with Mongolia, Cambodia, Nepal). 10 full SCO members: China, Russia, India, Pakistan, Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan, Uzbekistan, Iran (joined 2023 as 9th member), Belarus (joined 2024 as 10th member). SCO founded June 15, 2001 in Shanghai from Shanghai Five (1996); HQ: Beijing. Afghanistan's observer status reflects its geopolitical sensitivity — Taliban governance creates complications for consensus, though all SCO members have tacitly accepted Taliban rule post-2021. India prioritizes SCO cooperation on Afghanistan counterterrorism and Central Asia stability despite Afghan complications. Belarus's 2024 accession strengthened Russia's hand within SCO, offsetting India-Japan-USA partnerships and complicating consensus on Non-Aligned Movement issues.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21034,"question":"PM Modi outlined India's SCO vision at Tianjin 2025 under three pillars. Which is NOT one of those three?","option_a":"Security","option_b":"Connectivity","option_c":"Opportunity","option_d":"Democracy","correct_answer":"D","explanation":"PM Modi's three-pillar SCO vision at Tianjin 2025: (1) Security = regional counterterrorism, cybersecurity, maritime safety in shared waters; (2) Connectivity = infrastructure corridors (Central Asia to India, Belt-and-Road alternatives), digital infrastructure, supply-chain resilience; (3) Opportunity = shared economic growth, technology transfer, development cooperation, cultural exchange. 'Democracy' was NOT a pillar, reflecting India's pragmatic approach within SCO's mixed-regime membership (autocracies and democracies). Modi also proposed establishing a Civilizational Dialogue Forum within SCO to address shared cultural heritage, philosophical traditions (Buddhism, Hinduism, Islam), and people-to-people connectivity beyond state-to-state relations. This framework positions India as a bridge between Eurasian civilizations while advancing development priorities aligned with BRICS Global South agenda.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # ASEAN KL 2025 (21035-21040)
    {"id":21035,"question":"The 47th ASEAN Summit 2025 was held in which city, and which country chaired ASEAN in 2025?","option_a":"Bangkok, Thailand","option_b":"Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia","option_c":"Manila, Philippines","option_d":"Jakarta, Indonesia","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"47th ASEAN Summit was held in Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia on October 26-28, 2025 with Malaysia as ASEAN Chair. Malaysia's presidency prioritized ASEAN centrality in Indo-Pacific architecture, Myanmar crisis resolution, and economic integration amid China-USA strategic competition. Key outcomes: Timor-Leste admitted as 11th member (first expansion since Cambodia 1999), Thailand-Cambodia KL Peace Accord (Trump and PM Anwar witnessed), ASEAN-China FTA 3.0 Upgrade, South China Sea code-of-conduct progress, energy security, food security initiatives. India participated as a key dialogue partner, advancing Act East Policy and India-ASEAN maritime cooperation (QUAD-at-Sea operationalized June 2025). Chairmanship formally passed to Philippines (ASEAN alphabetical rotation). ASEAN now represents 11 member economies with ~700 million population across Indo-Pacific critical for India's strategic autonomy.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21036,"question":"Which country became the 11th member of ASEAN at the Kuala Lumpur Summit October 2025?","option_a":"Papua New Guinea","option_b":"Timor-Leste","option_c":"Sri Lanka","option_d":"Bangladesh","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Timor-Leste (East Timor) was formally admitted as ASEAN's 11th member at the 47th ASEAN Summit on October 26, 2025 — ASEAN's first expansion since Cambodia joined in 1999 (26-year gap). Timor-Leste gained independence from Indonesia on May 20, 2002 (strategic date symbolizing India's independence and ASEAN enlargement coordination), sought ASEAN membership since 2011 through multiple applications. Admission strengthens ASEAN's Pacific reach and maritime authority; Timor-Leste controls strategic chokepoints in Timor Sea oil/gas fields (disputed with Australia). India supports Timor-Leste as part of Act East Policy expansion and regional stability. ASEAN HQ: Jakarta, Indonesia. ASEAN now spans from Thailand/Cambodia westward through Indonesia/Philippines to Timor-Leste eastward, encompassing major Indo-Pacific sea lanes critical for India's maritime security.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21037,"question":"The 'Kuala Lumpur Peace Accord' signed at ASEAN 47th Summit 2025 was between which two nations?","option_a":"Myanmar and Thailand","option_b":"Thailand and Cambodia","option_c":"Vietnam and Cambodia","option_d":"Philippines and China","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Thailand and Cambodia signed the 'Kuala Lumpur Peace Accord' at the 47th ASEAN Summit 2025 (October 26, 2025) with President Trump and PM Anwar Ibrahim (Malaysia) as witnesses. Accord commits both nations to: permanent ceasefire along disputed border, withdrawal of heavy weapons from border, deployment of third-party observers for monitoring, and Bangkok releasing 18 Cambodian detainees. Thailand-Cambodia border tensions (pre-colonial kingdom disputes, 2008-2011 armed skirmishes) threatened regional stability; Trump's mediation signalled US renewed interest in Southeast Asian dispute resolution. India viewed accord as positive for ASEAN stability and demonstrated multilateral alternatives to bilateral antagonism. Trump simultaneously signed trade deals with Malaysia and Cambodia, establishing US economic presence as counter to Chinese economic dominance in Southeast Asia. ASEAN centrality in mediating intra-regional conflicts remains critical for Indo-Pacific order stability.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21038,"question":"ASEAN KL 2025 upgraded trade architecture with China through which agreement?","option_a":"ASEAN-China Comprehensive Trade Partnership","option_b":"ASEAN-China FTA 3.0 Upgrade Protocol","option_c":"RCEP 2.0 Enhanced Protocol","option_d":"ASEAN Plus Three Trade Modernisation Pact","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"ASEAN-China FTA 3.0 Upgrade Protocol was signed at ASEAN KL 2025 — deepening regional economic integration with China (ASEAN's largest external trade partner, ~20% of ASEAN trade in goods). The protocol modernized rules of origin, services liberalization, investment protections, and digital trade provisions from earlier versions. FTA evolution: Version 1.0 signed 2002 (early economic opening), Version 2.0 in 2015 (post-AEC launch), Version 3.0 in 2025 (digital, green trade, supply-chain resilience). India negotiated with ASEAN for India-ASEAN FTA upgrades to compete with China's economic dominance. ASEAN plays a hedging strategy between US-China competition, seeking maximum economic benefit from both while maintaining independence. China remains ASEAN's largest trading partner, supplier of manufactured goods, and investor in ASEAN infrastructure (Belt-and-Road Initiative).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21039,"question":"After 47th ASEAN Summit 2025, Malaysia handed the ASEAN Chairmanship to which country for 2026?","option_a":"Vietnam","option_b":"Thailand","option_c":"Philippines","option_d":"Indonesia","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Malaysia formally handed ASEAN Chairmanship to Philippines at the close of the 47th ASEAN Summit (October 28, 2025 closing ceremony). ASEAN Chair sequence follows alphabetical order by English country name: Laos (2024) → Malaysia (2025) → Philippines (2026) → Singapore (2027) → Thailand (2028) → Vietnam (2029). Philippines under President Ferdinand 'Bongbong' Marcos Jr. will chair 48th ASEAN Summit held in Cebu (May 6-8, 2026) under theme 'Navigating Our Future, Together.' ASEAN founded 1967 (Bangkok Declaration, ASEAN-5: Indonesia, Malaysia, Philippines, Singapore, Thailand); expanded to 10 members with Vietnam 1995, Laos/Myanmar 1997, Cambodia 1999; now 11 members after Timor-Leste joined 2025. India engages ASEAN through dialogue partnership and Act East Policy, seeking deeper strategic partnerships independent of US-China bipolarity.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21040,"question":"The ASEAN 47th Summit 2025 reviewed implementation of 'Five-Point Consensus' related to which member country's crisis?","option_a":"Cambodia","option_b":"Thailand","option_c":"Myanmar","option_d":"Vietnam","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"ASEAN's Five-Point Consensus (adopted April 2021, led by Singapore's PM Lee) addresses Myanmar's crisis following the February 1, 2021 military coup by Myanmar's Armed Forces (Tatmadaw). The five points: (1) dialogue through ASEAN mechanisms, (2) cessation of violence, (3) humanitarian assistance, (4) fact-finding mission, (5) special envoy to facilitate dialogue. At KL 2025, ASEAN leaders reaffirmed that cessation of violence MUST precede elections in Myanmar; agreed to maintain ASEAN Special Envoy (rotating annual appointment) for longer-term engagement. Myanmar remains ASEAN's most pressing challenge: military junta refuses to engage meaningfully, civil disobedience movement and armed resistance continue, humanitarian crisis deepens. India supports ASEAN's consensus approach while maintaining pragmatic engagement with Myanmar junta on security cooperation. Consensus-based ASEAN decision-making allows Myanmar participation despite coup, preventing Myanmar's exit while maintaining diplomatic pressure.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # COP30 Belem 2025 (21041-21048)
    {"id":21041,"question":"COP30 was held in which city in November 2025, symbolically located in the Amazon rainforest?","option_a":"São Paulo, Brazil","option_b":"Bogotá, Colombia","option_c":"Belém, Brazil","option_d":"Buenos Aires, Argentina","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"COP30 was held in Belém, Brazil (Pará state, Amazon mouth, November 10-21, 2025) — strategically located in the Amazon rainforest to emphasize deforestation as climate emergency. President Lula positioned Brazil as climate champion despite prior domestic critics of his environmental commitment. Key outcomes: 'Global Mutirão' (Brazilian Portuguese: collective community effort, mutual aid) call uniting humanity in climate action; $1.3 trillion/year climate finance by 2035 (vs COP29's paltry $300B); Tropical Forests Forever Facility (TFFF) launched with $6.7 billion pledged by 7 countries and endorsed by 53 nations; Loss & Damage Fund operationalized with $431.52M (far short of $724B/year need); tripling adaptation finance by 2035. India positioned as Global South voice, demanding equity-based climate action and technology transfer for developing nations. COP31 will be Antalya, Turkey 2026.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21042,"question":"The COP30 Belém 2025 climate finance decision sets a target to mobilise how much per year by 2035?","option_a":"$300 billion","option_b":"$500 billion","option_c":"$1 trillion","option_d":"$1.3 trillion","correct_answer":"D","explanation":"COP30 Belém adopted an unprecedented $1.3 trillion/year climate finance target by 2035 — a 4.3x increase over COP29 Baku's New Collective Quantified Goal (NCQG) of $300 billion/year. India, as lead voice for Global South, had called COP29's $300B 'paltry and inadequate,' arguing that climate damages in developing nations far exceed this (estimated $300-600B/year in adaptation needs alone). COP30's $1.3T target represented a major victory for Indian climate diplomacy and coalition-building with African, ASEAN, and SIDS nations. Financing structure: mitigation (30%), adaptation (50%), loss/damage (20%). COP30 also tripled adaptation finance by 2035 and operationalized the Loss & Damage Fund (FRLD) with initial $431.52M pledged. India positioned itself as leading the 'coalition of the vulnerable' — nations most affected by climate change despite lowest historical emissions.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21043,"question":"The Loss & Damage Fund (FRLD) became operational at COP30. What was its pledged amount as it opened for business?","option_a":"$1 billion","option_b":"$431 million","option_c":"$5 billion","option_d":"$10 billion","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"FRLD (Fund for Responding to Loss and Damage) became operational at COP30 with $431.52 million pledged — catastrophically short of the estimated $724 billion/year needed to address climate disasters in vulnerable nations. Pledging countries: Germany ($100M), USA ($50M), Denmark, Sweden, Fiji, South Korea, etc. The fund's glacial pace reflects developed-nation reluctance to acknowledge climate reparations as moral/legal obligation. Timeline: FRLD agreed at COP27 (Sharm el-Sheikh, Nov 2022), operationalized at COP28 (Dubai, Dec 2023), but opened for actual disbursement only at COP30. India advocated strongly for adequate FRLD funding as part of 'Common But Differentiated Responsibilities' (CBDR+RC) — principle that developed nations bear historical responsibility for emissions and must finance adaptation in developing nations. Small island developing states (SIDS) and LDCs (least developed countries) are FRLD's intended beneficiaries.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21044,"question":"COP30 Belém 2025 Political Package set what target for adaptation finance by 2035?","option_a":"Double","option_b":"Triple","option_c":"Quadruple","option_d":"Maintain current levels","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"COP30 adopted a target to TRIPLE adaptation finance by 2035 — signalling recognition that adaptation (helping countries cope with climate impacts) requires equal or greater priority than mitigation (reducing emissions). Adaptation finance helps developing nations manage climate impacts: extreme flooding (India monsoons), prolonged droughts (Sub-Saharan Africa), sea-level rise (SIDS, Bangladesh), heat waves (South Asia). Current adaptation finance: ~$30-50B/year, funded primarily through bilateral aid rather than multilateral mechanisms. COP29 Baku's $300B NCQG focused heavily on mitigation; COP30's tripling of adaptation represented advocacy victory by India, African nations, and SIDS for climate justice — developed nations caused climate change through historical emissions; developing nations suffer disproportionate impacts without responsibility. India emphasizes this equity gap as core to its G20 2023, BRICS 2024, and COP30 advocacy, positioning India as voice of 'climate vulnerable' nations.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21045,"question":"Which COP was the FIRST to include 'transition away from fossil fuels' language in its final text?","option_a":"COP26 Glasgow (2021)","option_b":"COP27 Sharm el-Sheikh (2022)","option_c":"COP28 Dubai (2023)","option_d":"COP29 Baku (2024)","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"COP28 Dubai (2023) was the first COP to include explicit 'transition away from fossil fuels' language in its final agreement — a historic breakthrough after nearly 30 years of climate negotiations. COP26 Glasgow (2021) addressed 'phase down,' not phase out. COP27 Sharm el-Sheikh (2022) created Loss & Damage Fund but avoided fossil fuel language due to OPEC opposition. COP28 language was carefully negotiated by UAE (COP host, oil-producing nation): 'transitioning away from fossil fuels in energy systems' without mandatory timeline, allowing flexibility. COP29 Baku (2024) set $300B/year New Collective Quantified Goal (NCQG) for climate finance. COP30 Belém (2025) raised climate finance to $1.3T/year by 2035 and tripled adaptation targets. India supported all fossil fuel language while advocating for Just Transition provisions protecting development rights of Global South nations dependent on coal/oil revenue.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21046,"question":"Where will COP31 be held in 2026?","option_a":"New Delhi, India","option_b":"Cairo, Egypt","option_c":"Antalya, Turkey","option_d":"Nairobi, Kenya","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"COP31 will be held in Antalya, Turkey in November 2026 (Australia sharing co-leadership role as Pacific perspective on climate). Turkey's selection reflects NATO alliance, geopolitical centrality (Black Sea, Middle East, Central Asia), and commitment to renewable energy transition (solar/wind capacity growth). COP host sequence: Dubai UAE (COP28, 2023) → Baku Azerbaijan (COP29, 2024) → Belém Brazil (COP30, 2025) → Antalya Turkey (COP31, 2026). India will be active participant in COP31, advancing climate justice demands, Loss & Damage Fund operationalization, and technology transfer mechanisms. After COP30's climate finance victories, COP31 focus likely: implementation mechanisms, Just Transition support, carbon markets (Article 6) finalization, nature-based solutions (NbS) for mitigation. UNFCCC Secretariat HQ: Bonn, Germany. COP process demonstrates Indian diplomatic leadership within Global South climate coalition.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21047,"question":"COP30 significance: Belém is located in which Brazilian state, at the mouth of which river?","option_a":"Mato Grosso; Pantanal wetlands","option_b":"Pará; Amazon River","option_c":"Amazonas; Rio Negro River","option_d":"Bahia; São Francisco River","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Belém is in Pará state, at the mouth (delta/estuary) of the Amazon River where it meets the Atlantic Ocean. Brazil's President Lula championed Belém as COP30 host to highlight Amazon deforestation threats and biodiversity conservation as core climate issues. The Amazon rainforest is a critical carbon sink: absorbs ~2 billion tons of CO2/year, stores 150-200 gigatons of carbon, home to ~10% of world's species, regulates regional rainfall patterns (supplying water to South American agriculture). Amazon faces tipping point: deforestation + degradation approaching 20-25% threshold beyond which forest converts to savanna (releasing stored carbon and accelerating warming). COP30 in Belém underscored urgency: Amazon protection is non-negotiable for global climate stability. India supports Tropical Forests Forever Facility (TFFF) launched at COP30 as mechanism to finance Amazon protection while respecting Brazil's sovereignty and development rights in Amazon region.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21048,"question":"COP30 'called on' countries to triple adaptation finance. What is the key LIMITATION noted by climate analysts?","option_a":"Applies only to G7 nations","option_b":"Doesn't specify baseline, funding sources, or target figure","option_c":"Requires unanimity to implement","option_d":"Excludes least developed countries","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"COP30's call to 'triple adaptation finance by 2035' has critical ambiguities: (1) No baseline specified — 'triple' from what? Current $30-50B/year or different estimate?; (2) No funding sources designated — developed nations, multilateral banks, private sector?; (3) No binding target figure — $90-150B/year is implied but not explicit; (4) No enforcement mechanism — calls are non-binding recommendations. This vagueness, while politically necessary to achieve consensus, weakens implementation. Climate analysts criticized Belém Political Package as emphasizing 'infrastructure of climate action' rather than delivering grand bargain on finance/technology. COP30's real victories: $1.3T global climate finance target (2035), TFFF launch with $6.7B pledged (though far short of Brazil's $125B goal), FRLD operationalization. India negotiated these as victories while acknowledging limitations, positioning itself as leading Global South voice in climate justice while maintaining constructive engagement with developed nations.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # WEF Davos 2025 & 2026 (21049-21052)
    {"id":21049,"question":"What was the theme of WEF Davos Annual Meeting 2025 (January 20-24, 2025)?","option_a":"Rebuilding Trust","option_b":"A Spirit of Dialogue","option_c":"Collaboration for the Intelligent Age","option_d":"Cooperation in a Fragmented World","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"'Collaboration for the Intelligent Age' was WEF Davos 2025 theme (55th Annual Meeting, January 20-24, 2025). AI dominated discourse: generative AI, automation, labor displacement, digital governance, tech regulation. Timing symbolically aligned with Trump's inauguration on January 20, coinciding with heightened US-China tech competition, geopolitical fragmentation, and questions about global cooperation feasibility. Davos 2025 attendance: ~2,500+ delegates across business, government, civil society, academia. India participated prominently: IT sector leadership (TCS, Infosys), policy representation on AI governance, climate finance advocacy. WEF theme progression: 2023='Cooperation in Fragmented World' → 2024='Rebuilding Trust' → 2025='Collaboration for Intelligent Age' → 2026='A Spirit of Dialogue.' Each reflects global challenges: fragmentation, trust deficit, AI acceleration, peace/dialogue urgency.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21050,"question":"What was the theme of WEF Davos Annual Meeting 2026 (January 19-23, 2026)?","option_a":"Collaboration for the Intelligent Age","option_b":"A Spirit of Dialogue","option_c":"Rebuilding Global Consensus","option_d":"Solidarity for a Sustainable Future","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"'A Spirit of Dialogue' was WEF Davos 2026 theme (56th Annual Meeting, January 19-23, 2026) — emphasizing urgent need for diplomatic dialogue amid geopolitical tensions (Russia-Ukraine, Iran-Israel-US war, China-Taiwan, India-Pakistan). Record attendance: 60+ heads of state/government, 400+ political leaders, 830+ CEOs, 2,700+ delegates. Key diplomatic moment: Zelenskyy met Trump bilaterally at Davos; both announced US-Ukraine-Russia trilateral peace talks in Abu Dhabi on January 23, 2026 (mediated by Pakistan). India participated with representation on AI governance, climate finance, development priorities. Theme reflected Davos organizers' perception that fragmentation and distrust had peaked; dialogue/bridge-building became urgent. This contrasted with 2025's 'Collaboration for Intelligent Age' (focused on technology), signalling shift toward conflict resolution and geopolitical de-escalation as central.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21051,"question":"At WEF Davos 2026, which significant Ukraine peace initiative was announced?","option_a":"Russia-Ukraine ceasefire signed in Davos","option_b":"US-Ukraine-Russia trilateral talks to begin in Abu Dhabi January 23","option_c":"NATO membership invitation for Ukraine","option_d":"China-mediated Ukraine peace roadmap","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"At Davos 2026 (January 19-23), Zelenskyy met Trump and announced US-Ukraine-Russia trilateral peace talks to begin in Abu Dhabi on January 23, 2026 — with Pakistan as mediator. This signified Trump's peace initiative emphasis and Zelenskyy's willingness to negotiate after 4+ years of war. Abu Dhabi talks focused on ceasefire terms, territorial arrangements, NATO membership status, reparations. Trump subsequently brokered a 3-day Russia-Ukraine ceasefire (May 9-11, 2026) with 1,000-prisoner exchanges each side — described as 'potentially the beginning of the end.' India maintained neutrality on Ukraine: critical of unilateral sanctions, opposed NATO expansion into non-aligned space, advocated for dialogue while acknowledging Russian aggression as violation of UN Charter. Davos 2026 signalled shift from military stalemate to negotiation, validating 'Spirit of Dialogue' theme.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21052,"question":"WEF's Future of Jobs Report predicted what fraction of current jobs will change by 2030, with 39% of skills becoming obsolete?","option_a":"1 in 8 jobs","option_b":"1 in 6 jobs","option_c":"1 in 4 jobs","option_d":"1 in 2 jobs","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"WEF Future of Jobs Report (2025): 1 in 4 current jobs (25%) will experience significant disruption by 2030; 39% of current skills will become obsolete within 5 years. Primary disruptors: AI/generative AI, automation, green energy transition, digital transformation, remote work normalization. Sectors most disrupted: administrative/clerical (highest displacement), manufacturing, finance, customer service. Counter-trends: healthcare, skilled trades, AI training/governance roles growing. India noted as having acute employment challenges: 12+ million annual workforce entrants competing for jobs with high AI displacement. WEF calls for reskilling initiatives (vocational education, lifelong learning, STEM emphasis) especially in Global South. India prioritizes tech/IT upskilling, coding academies, vocational training expansion to manage AI-driven labor market disruption.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # QUAD 2025 (21053-21056)
    {"id":21053,"question":"What happened to the QUAD Leaders' Summit scheduled to be hosted by India in 2025?","option_a":"Held successfully in New Delhi in November 2025","option_b":"No leaders' summit convened in 2025; rescheduled to 2026 but still not finalised as of May 2026","option_c":"Shifted from India to Japan","option_d":"Held virtually due to India-Pakistan tensions","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"India's 2025 QUAD Chairmanship (USA 2024 → India 2025 → Australia 2026 → Japan 2027) ended WITHOUT a formal leaders' summit — a significant diplomatic setback reflecting Trump's skepticism toward multilateral forums. Trump's return to presidency in January 2025 signalled deprioritization of QUAD as China-containment vehicle; Trump favored bilateral US-China relations and questioned value of QUAD commitments. EAM S. Jaishankar stated (February 2026) the QUAD summit was 'not cancelled' but unscheduled — diplomatic language indicating Trump's lack of enthusiasm. India pivoted to executing QUAD initiatives despite summit absence: MAITRI (maritime training), QUAD-at-Sea Ship Observer Mission (operationalized June 2025), Critical Minerals Initiative, Ports of the Future Forum (Mumbai, Oct 2025). QUAD FM meeting held in New Delhi (March 2026, US Secretary of State Rubio attending) was framed as leaders-level discussion, substituting for formal summit. This demonstrated India's flexibility in advancing QUAD agenda despite diminished Trump administration commitment.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21054,"question":"The 'QUAD-at-Sea Ship Observer Mission' was first operationalised in which month of 2025?","option_a":"March 2025","option_b":"June 2025","option_c":"September 2025","option_d":"December 2025","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"QUAD-at-Sea Ship Observer Mission was first operationalized in June 2025 — a mechanism placing rotating naval observers from India, USA, Japan, Australia aboard each other's naval vessels to enhance maritime interoperability, surveillance coordination, and real-time intelligence sharing. Observers rotate quarterly, experiencing each nation's naval operations, building trust, and establishing common protocols for Indo-Pacific maritime security. This was a major QUAD deliverable first announced at QUAD Wilmington Summit (September 2024) and prioritized by India's 2025 QUAD Chairmanship. The mission addresses China's naval expansion and grey-zone tactics (coast guard pressure, island-building, fishing-fleet militarization) in South China Sea and Indian Ocean. India led QUAD-at-Sea design, emphasizing respect for UNCLOS, freedom of navigation, and shared maritime interests. Mission represents operational QUAD cooperation advancing despite leadership summit absence.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21055,"question":"The QUAD 'Ports of the Future Partnership' was launched in 2025. Where was the conference hosted?","option_a":"New Delhi","option_b":"Tokyo","option_c":"Sydney","option_d":"Mumbai","correct_answer":"D","explanation":"QUAD Ports of the Future Partnership was launched at a major transportation/logistics conference in Mumbai, India (October 2025) — coinciding with India's ASEAN engagement and Indo-Pacific connectivity initiatives. The partnership focuses on: port modernization (green shipping, digital cargo tracking), port authority cooperation, maritime trade facilitation, supply-chain resilience against disruptions. India leveraged Mumbai's status as major Arabian Sea port and QUAD node to position itself as logistics hub for Indo-Pacific. QUAD simultaneously launched: Critical Minerals Initiative (2025) addressing supply-chain vulnerabilities for lithium/cobalt/rare earths critical for EV/renewable energy; QUAD STEM Scholarships (50 annual scholarships for Indo-Pacific students at Indian engineering institutions — IIT, NIT — advancing talent development and soft power). These 2025 QUAD initiatives (despite absent leaders' summit) demonstrated India's commitment to converting QUAD into operational framework advancing shared prosperity, maritime security, and technology cooperation.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21056,"question":"MAITRI (Maritime Initiative for Training in the Indo-Pacific) — a QUAD initiative — primarily does what?","option_a":"Conducts joint naval war games","option_b":"Trains Indo-Pacific partner nation coast guards","option_c":"Monitors Chinese naval movements","option_d":"Provides humanitarian disaster relief","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"MAITRI (Maritime Initiative for Training in the Indo-Pacific) trains and builds capacity of Indo-Pacific partner nation coast guards to enhance maritime security, law enforcement, and territorial protection. Program features: training workshops, equipment support, intelligence sharing, capacity assessments. Launched at QUAD Wilmington Summit (September 2024); first assessment workshop held 2025 identifying regional capability gaps in maritime domain awareness, maritime law enforcement, counter-piracy, IUU (illegal, unreported, unregulated) fishing suppression. Targets include ASEAN coast guards, Bangladesh, Sri Lanka, Maldives, Mauritius — nations vulnerable to Chinese maritime coercion (territorial disputes, fishing rights violations, debt-trap infrastructure). India leads MAITRI curriculum development, leveraging Indian Coast Guard's decades of Indo-Pacific operations. QUAD members: India, USA, Japan, Australia. QUAD formed 2007; revived 2017 as dialogue; elevated to leaders' summit level 2021. MAITRI exemplifies QUAD's operational pivot from dialogue to capacity-building delivery.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # India Bilateral & Others (21057-21062)
    {"id":21057,"question":"India and the UK formally signed the Comprehensive Economic and Trade Agreement (CETA) on which date in 2025?","option_a":"May 6, 2025","option_b":"July 24, 2025","option_c":"September 12, 2025","option_d":"November 15, 2025","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"India-UK CETA was formally signed July 24, 2025 following agreement-in-principle (May 6, 2025). Negotiations spanned three years (2022-2025) across goods, services, investment, IP, labor standards. Eliminates tariffs on 99% of Indian exports to UK (textiles, garments, seafood, pharmaceuticals, engineering goods); reduces tariffs on 90% of UK exports (whisky, medical devices, machinery). Expected to increase bilateral trade by $34 billion/year from 2040. Current bilateral trade: $56 billion; target: double by 2030. India positioned CETA as most significant FTA since India-UAE CEPA (2022). FTA reflects post-Brexit UK pivot toward Indo-Pacific partners and India's strategic autonomy in seeking diverse trade partnerships. India also advancing: EU-India FTA (signed Jan 27, 2026), GCC FTA, Canada FTA negotiations — demonstrating India's trade diplomacy diversification beyond China and US-centric partnerships.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21058,"question":"Under India-UK CETA 2025, what happens to UK tariffs on Indian whisky imports?","option_a":"Eliminated immediately to zero","option_b":"Halved from 150% to 75%, then reduced to 40% by year 10","option_c":"Reduced from 100% to 50% in year 5","option_d":"Maintained at current levels for strategic industries","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"India-UK CETA 2025: Whisky/gin (and similar spirit) tariffs phased: reduced from current 150% to 75% upon implementation, then to 40% by year 10 (gradual transition protecting Indian spirits industry). UK major export interests in CETA: whisky, premium spirits, medical devices/pharma machinery, electrical equipment, aerospace components, chocolate, salmon, lamb. Tariff reductions: UK gets 90% of Indian market with tariff elimination/reduction; India gets 99% of UK market duty-free. UK remains India's 5th largest trade partner (after USA, China, UAE, Singapore). CETA ratification expected to complete by May 2026; expected to come into force soon after. India views CETA as validation of India-UK Strategic Partnership (post-Brexit, UK seeks India deepening). Deal reflects India's negotiating strength: India gained more favorable terms on labor mobility, IP, FDI protections.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21059,"question":"India-UK CETA 2025 is expected to increase bilateral trade by how much per year from 2040?","option_a":"$10 billion","option_b":"$20 billion","option_c":"$34 billion","option_d":"$56 billion","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"India-UK CETA 2025 is projected to increase bilateral trade by $34 billion/year by 2040 — a substantial economic gain from tariff elimination and market access expansion. Current bilateral trade: $56 billion (2024); target: double to $112 billion by 2030 under CETA implementation. Added value from FTA realization: $34B/year increment. Considered India's most significant FTA since India-UAE CEPA (2022, then the largest in India's history). India-UK investment flows expected to increase substantially: UK pension funds, insurance, fintech firms seeking Indian market presence; Indian IT firms, pharma, chemicals expanding UK/EU operations. India also advancing parallel FTAs: EU-India FTA (signed Jan 27, 2026, larger than UK deal), GCC FTA, Canada FTA, UK FTA ratification expected May 2026. These FTAs position India as trade leader within Global South, countering China's bilateral dominance and validating India's strategic autonomy in diversifying economic partnerships.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21060,"question":"APEC 2025 was held in which South Korean city?","option_a":"Seoul","option_b":"Busan","option_c":"Gyeongju","option_d":"Incheon","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"APEC 2025 (33rd Economic Leaders' Meeting) was held in Gyeongju, South Korea (North Gyeongsang Province) in November 2025 — historically known as the 'museum without walls' preserving Silla dynasty (57 BCE-935 CE) heritage and Buddhist architecture. South Korean President Lee Jae-myung (elected June 3, 2025 in special presidential election following Yoon Suk Yeol's impeachment in December 2024) hosted as his first major international engagement. APEC prioritized tech innovation, supply-chain resilience post-pandemic, trade liberalization amid US protectionism, green transition. APEC host sequence: Lima Peru (2024) → Gyeongju South Korea (2025) → Shenzhen China (2026, announced Nov 1, 2025) → Phu Quoc Vietnam (2027). APEC: 21 member economies spanning East Asia, Southeast Asia, Americas, Oceania. India is dialogue partner; advocates for APEC openness on development issues.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21061,"question":"India-China resumed the Kailash Mansarovar Yatra in 2025 — suspended since 2020. The Yatra is a pilgrimage to which sacred site?","option_a":"Mount Kailash in Tibet, China","option_b":"Mansarovar Lake in Nepal","option_c":"Kedarnath in Uttarakhand, India","option_d":"Lhasa in Tibet Autonomous Region","correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Kailash Mansarovar Yatra: Hindu pilgrimage to Mount Kailash (6,638m, Himalayas in Tibet Autonomous Region, China) and Mansarovar Lake (4,590m) — sacred to Hindus (Shiva abode), Buddhists (spiritual center), Jains (Rishabhadeva's enlightenment site). Yatra suspended since 2020 due to COVID-19 and India-China border tensions (Galwan clash June 2020, 20 Indian soldiers killed). Resumption in 2025 was symbolic gesture of India-China normalization: bilateral LAC (Line of Actual Control) patrolling agreements finalized Oct-Nov 2024 (Depsang Plains, Demchok sectors); Modi-Xi meeting at SCO Tianjin 2025; commitment to resolve border disputes through dialogue. Yatra resumption signals confidence-building and people-to-people connectivity restoration. ~10,000+ Indian Hindu pilgrims annually visit Kailash; pilgrimage via Nathu La Pass (Sikkim) is operationally demanding but spiritually profound. Resumption demonstrates pragmatic India-China engagement despite unresolved border issues.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21062,"question":"EAM Jaishankar met Iranian FM Araghchi in New Delhi on May 15, 2026, during which ongoing international conflict?","option_a":"Russia-Ukraine War","option_b":"Sudan Civil War","option_c":"2026 Iran-US-Israel War","option_d":"India-Pakistan Operation Sindoor aftermath","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"EAM S. Jaishankar met Iranian FM Abbas Araghchi (May 15, 2026) during the 2026 Iran War — US+Israel military campaign against Iran initiated February 28, 2026 following Iranian drone/missile strikes on Israel. War escalation: Israeli air strikes on Iranian military infrastructure, nuclear facilities; Iranian retaliation via Hezbollah (Lebanon), Houthis (Yemen), Iraqi militias. Pakistan-brokered ceasefire agreement signed April 8, 2026; Israel violated ceasefire by striking Beirut on May 7, 2026. India maintained strategic neutrality during conflict: (1) protected Chabahar Port (Iranian trade gateway, India-invested) from sanctions/strikes; (2) maintained dialogue with both Iran and Israel; (3) advocated multilateral ceasefire through UNSC; (4) avoided alignment with US containment of Iran, respecting India's non-aligned tradition. EAM visit to Tehran signaled India's commitment to Iran partnership despite geopolitical pressures, validating India's independent foreign policy.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # 2026 Upcoming Events (21063-21066)
    {"id":21063,"question":"India will host the 18th BRICS Summit in 2026. When and where is it scheduled?","option_a":"June 2026, Mumbai","option_b":"September 12-13, 2026, New Delhi","option_c":"October 2026, Hyderabad","option_d":"December 2026, Bengaluru","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"18th BRICS Summit 2026: New Delhi, September 12-13, 2026 — India's 2026 BRICS Chairmanship (first India chair since expansion to 11 members + 10 partners). This is India's second BRICS summit chair (previous: 2016, Goa — when BRICS still 5 members). India's expected priorities: (1) South-South cooperation and development finance mechanisms, (2) BRICS Development Bank expansion, (3) technology transfer for green energy transition, (4) counterterrorism cooperation (Pahalgam attack condemnation model), (5) de-dollarization alternatives to USD (BRICS reserve currency discussion), (6) inclusive development balancing China's Belt-and-Road with India's Partnership for Global Growth vision. New Delhi summit will validate India's diplomatic leadership within Global South and showcase India's capacity to manage diverse BRICS consensus from ideological alignment to operational cooperation.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21064,"question":"CHOGM 2026 (Commonwealth Heads of Government Meeting) will be held in which country?","option_a":"Australia","option_b":"Kenya","option_c":"Antigua and Barbuda","option_d":"Canada","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"CHOGM 2026: St. John's, Antigua and Barbuda (Caribbean nation, population ~97,000) on November 1-4, 2026. Theme: 'Accelerating Partnerships and Investment for a Prosperous Commonwealth.' Antigua and Barbuda Prime Minister Gaston Browne hosting. Previous CHOGM 2024: Apia, Samoa (October 25-26, 2024, first in Pacific island state); Commonwealth leaders adopted Apia Ocean Declaration affirming maritime boundaries as fixed despite sea-level rise. Commonwealth Head of State: King Charles III (UK, ceremonial). Secretariat: Marlborough House, London. 56 member nations spanning Africa, Asia, Caribbean, Pacific, Americas. India participates as founding Commonwealth member (1949), advancing South-South cooperation, development priorities, climate justice, technology transfer agendas within Commonwealth forums. CHOGM 2026 expected to emphasize SIDS vulnerability to climate change and demand for loss-and-damage financing.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21065,"question":"Where will the 2026 Asian Games be held?","option_a":"Beijing, China","option_b":"Doha, Qatar","option_c":"Aichi-Nagoya, Japan","option_d":"Seoul, South Korea","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"2026 Asian Games: Aichi-Nagoya, Japan (Chubu region, central Honshu) in October 2026 — second Asian Games in Japan (Tokyo hosted 2020 Olympics/Paralympics; Sapporo hosted 1972 Winter Olympics). Aichi hosts 15 sports across 8 venues. India competitive in: athletics, badminton, shooting, wrestling, weightlifting; target 50+ medals. Other major 2026 sports events: ICC Men's T20 World Cup (India & Sri Lanka co-hosts, June 2026); 2026 Commonwealth Games (Glasgow, Scotland, July 28-Aug 8); 2026 Summer Youth Olympics (Dakar, Senegal, October-November); 2026 FIFA World Cup (USA-Canada-Mexico, June-July). India last hosted Asian Games: New Delhi 1982 (where India won 13 medals). 2026 Asian Games represents India's continued commitment to regional sports cooperation and soft power in Asia-Pacific.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21066,"question":"The 2026 FIFA World Cup will be co-hosted by which three countries?","option_a":"USA, Canada, Mexico","option_b":"USA, Brazil, Argentina","option_c":"UK, France, Germany","option_d":"Spain, Portugal, Morocco","correct_answer":"A","explanation":"2026 FIFA World Cup: USA, Canada, Mexico (June-July 2026) — historic first World Cup with three co-host nations (124 matches across 16 stadiums in North America). Also the first 48-team World Cup (expanded from 32 teams), increasing competition inclusivity. 2022 FIFA World Cup was in Qatar (first in Middle East); Argentina defeated France in final. 2026 will be the first World Cup in North America since USA 1994 (USA hosted, Brazil won). India fielded women's national team in 2023 FIFA World Cup (failed to progress from group stage); men's team historically failed qualification rounds. India focuses on youth development and women's football growth, supported by IMG-Reliance partnership (Indian Super League) and grassroots academy expansion. 2026 World Cup symbolizes globalizing football in non-traditional markets (Mexico historically a strong football nation, Canada/USA emerging as growth markets).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # Mixed / Higher Order (21067-21080)
    {"id":21067,"question":"Which of the following correctly matches the 2025 summit with its HOST city?","option_a":"G20 2025 → Rio de Janeiro","option_b":"NATO 2025 → The Hague","option_c":"SCO 2025 → Astana","option_d":"ASEAN 2025 → Bangkok","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"NATO 2025 → The Hague ✓ (June 24-25, 2025). Incorrect matches: (a) G20 2025 → Johannesburg, not Rio (Rio hosted BRICS 2025), (c) SCO 2025 → Tianjin, not Astana (Astana hosted SCO 2024), (d) ASEAN 2025 → Kuala Lumpur, not Bangkok. Complete 2025 summit roster: G20=Johannesburg (SA), G7=Kananaskis (Canada), NATO=The Hague (Netherlands), BRICS=Rio (Brazil), SCO=Tianjin (China), ASEAN=Kuala Lumpur (Malaysia), COP30=Belém (Brazil), WEF Davos 2025=January 20-24, WEF Davos 2026=January 19-23. This question tests memorization and global awareness of multilateral forum scheduling — critical for diplomacy, policy planning, and geopolitical analysis.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21068,"question":"Which of the following CORRECTLY matches 2025 summit themes?","option_a":"G20='Collaboration for Intelligent Age'; WEF='Solidarity, Equality, Sustainability'","option_b":"G20='A Spirit of Dialogue'; WEF='Solidarity, Equality, Sustainability'","option_c":"G20='Solidarity, Equality, Sustainability'; WEF='Collaboration for Intelligent Age'","option_d":"G20='Solidarity, Equality, Sustainability'; WEF='A Spirit of Dialogue'","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"CORRECT: G20 South Africa 2025 = 'Solidarity, Equality, Sustainability' (Ubuntu-rooted, emphasizing Global South priorities). WEF Davos 2025 = 'Collaboration for the Intelligent Age' (AI-centric). Others incorrect: (a) swaps G20-WEF themes, (b) WEF 2026 theme, (d) mixes G20 correctly with WEF 2026. Key differentiator: G20 SA chose African Ubuntu philosophy to center Global South; WEF focused on AI/tech acceleration. Common confusion source: both summits happened in 2025 and both address development, but with opposing framings (Ubuntu solidarity vs AI collaboration). WEF Davos 2026 later adopted 'A Spirit of Dialogue' reflecting urgent need for peace/diplomacy. This question tests detailed knowledge of summit diplomacy and geopolitical messaging priorities.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21069,"question":"Which statement about NATO Hague Summit 2025 is INCORRECT?","option_a":"New defense spending target of 5% GDP was agreed","option_b":"Spain was the only dissenter to the 5% target","option_c":"Ukraine was officially invited to join NATO","option_d":"€40 billion in defense aid was pledged to Ukraine","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"INCORRECT: Ukraine was NOT invited to join NATO at The Hague 2025 — no membership invitation, formal timeline, or upgraded pathway was granted. NATO deliberately avoided formal membership offer to prevent direct NATO-Russia military clash (Article 5 trigger risk). Correct facts: (a) 5% GDP defense target agreed by 31 of 32 members, (b) Spain sole dissenter, (d) €40B Ukraine aid pledge. NATO prioritized supporting Ukraine militarily while maintaining escalation deterrence through non-membership. Ukraine's strategic ambiguity (seeking NATO membership for security assurance while not receiving it) reflects Western caution about Russia escalation during ongoing conflict. NATO 2026: Ankara, Turkey (July 7-8). India observed NATO expansion as validation for India's own defense modernization while maintaining neutrality on Ukraine conflict per non-aligned tradition.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21070,"question":"Which country is BOTH ASEAN's newest member AND BRICS's newest full member?","option_a":"Vietnam","option_b":"Malaysia","option_c":"No such country — they are different nations","option_d":"Indonesia","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"CORRECT: These are DIFFERENT nations. ASEAN's newest member = Timor-Leste (admitted October 2025, independent since 2002). BRICS's newest full member = Indonesia (joined January 6, 2025 as 11th member). Indonesia was ASEAN's founding member (1967, original ASEAN-5); became BRICS member 2025, not a new ASEAN member. Timor-Leste joined ASEAN but is NOT in BRICS (Pakistan, Iran, Afghanistan remain outside both despite strategic proximity). This question tests careful reading and knowledge of multilateral organization memberships — critical for diplomacy. It highlights ASEAN and BRICS as separate, non-overlapping formations with distinct membership criteria and strategic purposes.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21071,"question":"The Russia-Ukraine war saw a Trump-brokered ceasefire in May 2026. What was its duration?","option_a":"1 day","option_b":"3 days (May 9-11, 2026)","option_c":"7 days","option_d":"30 days","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Trump announced a 3-day Russia-Ukraine ceasefire (May 9-11, 2026), formally described as 'the beginning of the end' of the war's major combat phase. Terms: mutual ceasefire on all fronts, exchange of 1,000+ prisoners/detainees each side, deployment of international monitors, preliminary negotiations for territorial arrangements/reparations. Russia simultaneously declared unilateral ceasefire for Victory Day (May 8-9, commemorating Soviet WWII victory). Both sides immediately traded blame for ceasefire violations; sporadic shelling continued despite pause. Ceasefire reflected war fatigue after 4+ years (Feb 2022-May 2026): Ukraine faced manpower depletion, Western aid fatigue; Russia faced economic costs, equipment losses. Trump's mediation signalled shift from military stalemate to negotiation, validating Davos 2026 'Spirit of Dialogue' theme. India welcomed ceasefire as step toward negotiations while maintaining neutrality on conflict origins/responsibility.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21072,"question":"India-UK CETA 2025: What percentage of Indian exports to UK will get duty-free access?","option_a":"80%","option_b":"90%","option_c":"95%","option_d":"99%","correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Under India-UK CETA 2025, 99% of Indian exports to UK receive duty-free market access (covering ~100% of bilateral trade value on merchandise goods). UK reciprocal access: 90% of UK exports to India benefit from tariff elimination/reduction. India's major export beneficiaries: textiles/garments (UK's largest import source), footwear, processed seafood, pharmaceuticals/chemicals, engineering goods, gemstones. UK major exports gaining access: whisky/premium spirits, medical devices/diagnostics, electrical machinery, aerospace components, high-value food (salmon, lamb, cheese, chocolate). CETA ratification expected Parliamentary approval by May 2026, entry into force shortly after. Trade impact: projected $34B additional annual trade by 2040. CETA represents India's strategic pivot toward advanced democracies and post-Brexit UK seeking Indo-Pacific partners, validating India's diplomatic leverage in securing favorable FTA terms.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21073,"question":"After 2025 expansions, how many total nations (full members + partner countries) does BRICS represent?","option_a":"15","option_b":"18","option_c":"21","option_d":"24","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"BRICS after 2025: 11 full members + 10 partner countries = 21 total nations. Full members (voting): Brazil, Russia, India, China, South Africa (original 5) + Egypt, Ethiopia, Iran, UAE, Saudi Arabia (joined 2024) + Indonesia (joined Jan 6, 2025). Partner countries (non-voting): Belarus, Bolivia, Kazakhstan, Cuba, Malaysia, Nigeria, Thailand, Uganda, Uzbekistan, Vietnam (admitted Rio 2025). Two-tier structure created to accommodate expansion pressures while maintaining decision-making efficiency. Collective GDP (PPP): ~37% of world total (exceeding G7's 30%). Collective population: 45%+ of world (~3.5 billion people). This represents unprecedented Global South institutional power — rivaling or exceeding Western-dominated institutions (IMF, World Bank, UNSC). India leverages BRICS 2026 Chairmanship to deepen Global South consensus on development finance, technology transfer, climate justice.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21074,"question":"SCO was founded in which year and city?","option_a":"1996, Almaty","option_b":"2001, Shanghai","option_c":"2005, Beijing","option_d":"1997, Moscow","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"SCO (Shanghai Cooperation Organisation) formally founded June 15, 2001 in Shanghai, China — the founding city naming the organization. SCO evolved from 'Shanghai Five' (informal grouping, 1996: China, Russia, Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan focused on border security/confidence-building) when Uzbekistan joined 2001, formalizing into SCO. Membership expansion: India and Pakistan joined 2017 (9th/10th members); Iran joined 2023 as full member (9th); Belarus joined 2024 (10th full member). Observer status: Afghanistan, Mongolia, Cambodia, Nepal. SCO Secretariat HQ: Beijing, China; Regional Anti-Terrorism Structure (RATS): Tashkent, Uzbekistan. SCO covers ~45% world population and major Eurasian land mass. India values SCO as Central Asian security mechanism, counterterrorism cooperation framework, and multilateral alternative to US-led alliances. SCO symbolizes post-Cold War Eurasian integration independent of Western institutions.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21075,"question":"Which countries called the COP29 NCQG of $300 billion/year 'paltry and inadequate'?","option_a":"USA and EU","option_b":"China and Russia","option_c":"India and other developing nations","option_d":"Saudi Arabia and OPEC nations","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"India and the Global South coalition (African nations, SIDS, AOSIS) called the COP29 Baku NCQG (New Collective Quantified Goal) of $300 billion/year 'paltry and inadequate.' India FM Jaishankar and other developing-nation representatives walked out of COP29 negotiations, objecting that $300B falls short of $1-3 trillion needed for climate adaptation/mitigation in Global South. COP30 Belém 2025 effectively adopted India's demand: $1.3 trillion/year by 2035 climate finance target — a major diplomatic victory for the developing-nation coalition. India argues for equity-based 'Common But Differentiated Responsibilities + Respective Capabilities' (CBDR+RC) principle: developed nations historically emitted 80% of atmospheric CO2 (1850-2020); must finance Global South climate adaptation proportionally. India's climate justice advocacy positions it as leading voice for vulnerable nations.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21076,"question":"Which summit in 2025 involved a formal chairmanship handover at its closing ceremony to a new host?","option_a":"G20 (South Africa → USA)","option_b":"ASEAN (Malaysia → Philippines)","option_c":"SCO (China → Kazakhstan)","option_d":"BRICS (Brazil → India)","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"ASEAN KL 2025 (October 26-28): Malaysia formally handed ASEAN Chairmanship to Philippines at the closing ceremony. ASEAN follows strict alphabetical rotation (English country names) for annual chairing: Laos (2024) → Malaysia (2025) → Philippines (2026) → Singapore (2027) → Thailand (2028) → Vietnam (2029). This ensures predictable governance and shared responsibility across all 11 members. Incorrect options: (a) G20 handover occurred offsite (Jan 12, 2026), not at summit; (c) SCO operates differently, with China as de facto leader (hosting Secretariat); (d) BRICS Chairmanship Brazil → India for 2026 also a formal transfer but occurred post-Rio summit. ASEAN's ceremonial handover at summit closing symbolizes institutional stability and commitment to consensus governance across geopolitically diverse membership.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21077,"question":"The 2026 Commonwealth Games will be held in which city?","option_a":"Brisbane, Australia","option_b":"Dublin, Ireland","option_c":"Glasgow, Scotland","option_d":"Durban, South Africa","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"2026 Commonwealth Games: Glasgow, Scotland (July 28-August 8, 2026) — Glasgow's second hosting (previously 2014 XX Commonwealth Games). 2026 focuses on gender equality (equal medals for men/women events) and sustainability (minimal new construction, repurposing 2014 venues). Previous hosts: 2022 Birmingham (UK), 2018 Gold Coast (Australia), 2014 Glasgow. India hosted: New Delhi 2010 (XIX Commonwealth Games, large-scale success). Glasgow's second hosting is rare in Commonwealth Games history, reflecting Scotland's developed infrastructure and post-COVID financial recovery. India expected to be competitive across athletics, badminton, shooting, weightlifting, wrestling with 150+ athletes. Commonwealth Games represents India's continued soft power within Commonwealth family (56 nations) and democratic multilateral engagement alongside BRICS/SCO forums.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21078,"question":"G7 Kananaskis 2025 was the 51st G7 Summit. Which of the following is the correct list of G7 nations?","option_a":"USA, UK, France, Germany, Italy, Japan, Canada","option_b":"USA, UK, France, Germany, Italy, Japan, Australia","option_c":"USA, UK, France, Germany, Italy, Spain, Canada","option_d":"USA, UK, France, Germany, Russia, Japan, Canada","correct_answer":"A","explanation":"G7 members: USA, UK, France, Germany, Italy, Japan, Canada + European Union (special participant, not voting member). Incorrect: Australia (ally but not member; participates in Indo-Pacific extension), Spain (NATO/EU but not G7 — Germany represents EU interests), Russia (suspended from G8 in 2014 after Crimea annexation, reverting to G7). G7 represents advanced Western democracies (~50% global GDP, ~1 billion population). Russia's exclusion reflects Western response to territorial aggression. At Kananaskis 2025, PM Mark Carney hosted; Trump's early departure signalled weakening Western cohesion. India observes G7 as Western bloc institution; seeks plurilateral alternatives (BRICS, SCO, QUAD, I2U2) to represent Global South interests independent of G7 dominance.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21079,"question":"BRICS Rio 2025 highlighted de-dollarisation debates. The original BRIC concept was proposed in which year by Goldman Sachs economist Jim O'Neill?","option_a":"1999","option_b":"2001","option_c":"2003","option_d":"2005","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Goldman Sachs economist Jim O'Neill coined 'BRIC' acronym in 2001 in report 'Building Better Global Economic BRICs' — predicting Brazil, Russia, India, China would dominate global economic growth by 2050, surpassing G7 by 2020s. BRIC grouping first met formally 2009 (Yekaterinburg, Russia); South Africa joined 2010 (making it BRICS). De-dollarization at Rio 2025 reflects BRICS's foundational thesis: emerging economies should possess financial autonomy independent of USD hegemony. BRICS now exploring alternatives: BRICS common currency, yuan internationalization, bilateral trade in local currencies, unified payment systems bypassing SWIFT. Concept originated 24 years prior (2001-2025), validating O'Neill's prescience. India leverages BRICS 2026 to advance de-dollarization while maintaining USD transaction compatibility — balanced approach protecting trade stability.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21080,"question":"ASEAN's newest member Timor-Leste (joined 2025) gained independence from which country in 2002?","option_a":"Portugal","option_b":"Australia","option_c":"Indonesia","option_d":"Netherlands","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Timor-Leste (East Timor) gained independence from Indonesia on May 20, 2002 — a symbolically important date coinciding with India's Independence Day, representing decolonization and sovereignty restoration across developing nations. Timor-Leste history: former Portuguese colony (1520s-1974, Portuguese official language), occupied by Indonesia (1975-1999, following Portugal's decolonization), UN-administered transition (1999-2002 after referendum), independence 2002. Applied for ASEAN membership in 2011; finally admitted October 26, 2025 at 47th ASEAN Summit in Kuala Lumpur as 11th member — ASEAN's first expansion in 26 years (since Cambodia 1999). Timor-Leste's late ASEAN accession reflected concerns over capacity, development levels, and consensus-building burden. May 20 independence date holds geopolitical significance: shared with India's independence commemoration, representing developing-nation sovereign assertion against colonialism/imperialism.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},

    # ── Freshness Gap-Fill May 19, 2026 (21081-21100) ──
    {"id":21081,"question":"At the 16th EU-India Summit on January 27, 2026, India and the EU concluded which historic agreement, described by PM Modi as the 'mother of all deals'?","option_a":"EU-India Defence Procurement Pact","option_b":"EU-India Free Trade Agreement (FTA)","option_c":"EU-India Digital Single Market Agreement","option_d":"EU-India Green Hydrogen Partnership","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"India-EU FTA signed January 27, 2026 — India's largest and most comprehensive FTA, concluding 17 years of negotiations (2009-2026). PM Modi called it the 'mother of all deals' reflecting scope: goods (tariff elimination on 95% of trade), services, investment, government procurement, IP, sustainability. António Costa (European Council President) and Ursula von der Leyen (Commission President) were Chief Guests at India's 77th Republic Day (Jan 26, 2026). Additional pacts signed simultaneously: EU-India Security & Defence Partnership (addressing Russia, China containment), Mobility & Migration Agreement (facilitating student/professional visas). FTA projected to increase bilateral trade by 50%+ from current €80-100B annually. India gains market access for textiles, pharmaceuticals, chemicals, engineering; EU gains automotive, machinery, dairy. This validates India-EU strategic autonomy from US-centric partnerships.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21082,"question":"Who represented the EU at the 16th EU-India Summit in New Delhi (January 25-27, 2026) — Chief Guests for India's 77th Republic Day?","option_a":"Charles Michel and Roberta Metsola","option_b":"António Costa and Ursula von der Leyen","option_c":"Josep Borrell and Christine Lagarde","option_d":"Olaf Scholz and Emmanuel Macron","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"António Costa (President of European Council, since December 1, 2024) and Ursula von der Leyen (President of European Commission, second term 2024-2029) led the 16-member EU delegation to India as Chief Guests at India's 77th Republic Day Parade (January 26, 2026). EU-India Summit duration: January 25-27, 2026 in New Delhi culminating in FTA signing. Costa's selection as EU Council President (replacing Charles Michel) represented new European leadership recognizing India's strategic importance post-Brexit, amid NATO expansion, and in response to Trump's transactionalism. Von der Leyen's second term emphasizes EU-India strategic autonomy, green transition cooperation, defense coordination. Their joint presence signalled EU's commitment to repositioning India as strategic partner rather than development-aid recipient — validating India's diplomatic advancement.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21083,"question":"What was unprecedented about the adoption of the G20 Johannesburg Leaders' Declaration on November 22, 2025?","option_a":"It was adopted unanimously by all 21 members","option_b":"It was adopted at the START of the summit (not at the end), breaking G20 custom","option_c":"It was the longest declaration in G20 history","option_d":"It was the first to mention climate change","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"President Cyril Ramaphosa broke with G20 convention by tabling the 122-point Leaders' Declaration at the OPENING session of the summit (November 22, 2025) — not the closing ceremony as is traditional. Adopted without objection by all 20 present members (USA absent). Ramaphosa's procedural innovation served strategic purpose: pre-declaring outcomes prevented US absence from derailing consensus; established that multilateral outcomes proceed without Western hegemon participation. 122-point declaration addressed: Ubuntu philosophy, UNSC reform, climate finance, development agenda, critical minerals, Africa development, debt sustainability, Mission 300 electricity access, AI governance. Unprecedented procedural maneuver signalled South Africa's diplomatic assertiveness and Global South determination to advance multilateralism independent of G7/US consensus—validating India's own G20 2023 approach prioritizing Global South outcomes over Western accommodation.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21084,"question":"At CHOGM Samoa 2024, Commonwealth leaders adopted the 'Apia Commonwealth Ocean Declaration'. What is one of its key novel commitments?","option_a":"Banning all deep-sea mining by 2030","option_b":"Recognising maritime boundaries as FIXED even if islands are submerged by sea level rise","option_c":"Mandatory 50% renewable energy by 2035","option_d":"A Commonwealth Climate Fund worth £100 billion","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Apia Ocean Declaration (CHOGM Samoa, October 25-26, 2024) established groundbreaking principle: maritime boundaries/EEZ (Exclusive Economic Zones) remain FIXED under international law (UNCLOS) even if sea-level rise submerges island nations. Protects SIDS fishing rights, continental shelf claims, resource extraction rights despite climate change impacts. King Charles III presided (first CHOGM under his reign). Declaration notably avoided deep-sea mining bans (mining-state opposition). Apia is Samoa's capital (Polynesia); declaration location symbolically emphasized island nation vulnerability. India supports SIDS maritime rights through Commonwealth and AOSIS forums, validating climate justice principle that vulnerable nations should not lose sovereignty due to climate damages caused by developed-nation emissions. Apia Declaration operationalizes ocean governance in age of rising seas.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21085,"question":"The Tropical Forests Forever Facility (TFFF) launched at COP30 Belém (Nov 6, 2025) raised approximately how much in pledges by the close of COP30?","option_a":"$1.3 billion","option_b":"$2.8 billion","option_c":"$6.7 billion","option_d":"$12 billion","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"TFFF raised $6.7 billion in pledges by COP30 close (Nov 21, 2025) from Brazil, Indonesia, Norway, France, Portugal, Netherlands, Germany, and endorsed by 53 countries globally. TFFF mechanism: direct transfers to forest-protecting countries contingent on forest protection/restoration; 20% payments mandatory to Indigenous Peoples & local communities (whose traditional stewardship maintains ~80% intact forests). Brazil's ultimate ambition: $125B by 2035 (initial phase: $10B from governments + philanthropies). TFFF addresses deforestation crisis: Amazon loses ~10,000 hectares daily; tropical forests store 250-300 gigatons carbon (2x global annual emissions). India supported TFFF as climate finance mechanism respecting national sovereignty while incentivizing forest conservation. Unlike top-down REDD+ (carbon markets), TFFF provides guaranteed financing independent of carbon pricing volatility.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21086,"question":"COP30's main political decision called for humanity to unite in a 'Global ______' against climate change — a Portuguese word meaning collective effort.","option_a":"Mutirão","option_b":"Saudade","option_c":"Carnaval","option_d":"Quilombo","correct_answer":"A","explanation":"'Global Mutirão' was COP30's flagship political messaging — 'mutirão' is a Brazilian-Portuguese word meaning collective community effort, shared labor, mutual aid (Indigenous + African diaspora tradition). Frames climate action as humanity's shared responsibility requiring solidarity across Global North-South divides rather than moral lectures from developed nations. Concept resonates with Indigenous land-stewardship traditions and Brazilian culture. The Belém Political Package + Mutirão call were headline outcomes alongside $1.3T climate finance, TFFF launch, FRLD operationalization, tripling adaptation finance. 'Global Mutirão' contrasts with competitive climate metrics (carbon trading, national targets), emphasizing collaborative survival. India embraced Mutirão concept, aligning it with 'Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam' (world is one family) and Ubuntu principles — validating Indian philosophical traditions as legitimate climate governance frameworks. COP30 language shift (from 'commitments' to 'collective effort') reflects recognition that climate crisis requires transcending national interest calculations.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21087,"question":"The 11th Raisina Dialogue (March 5-7, 2026) had which world leader as Chief Guest and keynote speaker?","option_a":"French President Emmanuel Macron","option_b":"Finnish President Alexander Stubb","option_c":"Brazilian President Lula da Silva","option_d":"Greek PM Kyriakos Mitsotakis","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Finland President Alexander Stubb was Chief Guest at the 11th Raisina Dialogue (March 5-7, 2026, New Delhi); inaugurated by PM Modi on March 5. Stubb visited India March 4-7. Attended by representatives from 110 nations. Raisina Dialogue is India's premier geopolitics/geoeconomics multilateral forum, organized jointly by MEA (Ministry of External Affairs) and Observer Research Foundation (ORF) since 2016. Stubb's keynote emphasized EU-India strategic partnership, Nordic security experience (Russia threats), and democratic values in multipolar world. Finland's presidency represents Nordic model influence in India's foreign policy calculations. Raisina 2026 themes: geopolitical fragmentation, US-China-India triangle, Russia-Ukraine resolution prospects, climate finance, AI governance, Indo-Pacific architecture. Event validates India as thought-leader on global governance and strategic autonomy.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21088,"question":"APEC 2026 — the 33rd Economic Leaders' Meeting — will be hosted by which country and where?","option_a":"South Korea, Busan","option_b":"China, Shenzhen","option_c":"Vietnam, Phu Quoc","option_d":"USA, San Francisco","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"APEC 2026: Shenzhen, China (Guangdong Province, economic special zone) on November 18-19, 2026 — announced by President Xi Jinping on November 1, 2025. Third time China hosts APEC (Shanghai 2001 as founding summit, Beijing 2014). Theme: 'Building an Asia-Pacific Community to Prosper Together.' Shenzhen's selection symbolizes China's economic leadership within APEC (21 member economies, 60% global GDP, 50% global trade). Host sequence: Gyeongju South Korea (2025) → Shenzhen China (2026) → Phu Quoc Vietnam (2027). India is APEC dialogue partner; uses forum to advance development agenda and Indo-Pacific connectivity independent of China dominance. APEC 2026 likely to address: US-China trade tensions, digital economy standards, supply-chain resilience, green transition financing.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21089,"question":"The 48th ASEAN Summit 2026 was held in which Philippine city, and what was its theme?","option_a":"Manila — 'A Decade of ASEAN Centrality'","option_b":"Cebu — 'Navigating Our Future, Together'","option_c":"Davao — 'Sustainable Southeast Asia'","option_d":"Iloilo — 'Unity Through Diversity'","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"48th ASEAN Summit held in Cebu City (Visayas region, central Philippines) on May 6-8, 2026 under theme 'Navigating Our Future, Together.' Philippines holds ASEAN Chairmanship 2026 (President Ferdinand 'Bongbong' Marcos Jr.). Originally planned 5-day summit compressed to 3 days for efficiency. Key priorities: energy security (renewable transition), food security (agricultural cooperation), safety of ASEAN nationals abroad (labor protections, trafficking). Cebu's selection reflects its status as ASEAN's 2nd-largest metro and transportation hub. Summit addressed Myanmar crisis persistence (ASEAN Five-Point Consensus status), South China Sea tensions (code-of-conduct progress), Indo-Pacific architecture. India participated as dialogue partner, advancing maritime security cooperation (QUAD-at-Sea model for ASEAN). ASEAN 2026 prioritized consolidating Timor-Leste's membership and strengthening regional cohesion amid geopolitical pressures.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21090,"question":"At WEF Davos 2026 (January 22, 2026), Trump formally launched the 'Board of Peace'. What was its primary initial focus?","option_a":"Russia-Ukraine peace mediation","option_b":"Reconstruction of Gaza","option_c":"Indo-Pacific security architecture","option_d":"Arctic governance","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Trump's 'Board of Peace' (Gaza Peace Council) was formally ratified at Davos 2026 (January 22) — primary focus: reconstruction & governance of Gaza post-2024-2025 Israel-Hamas war. Formally approved via UNSC Resolution 2803 (November 17, 2025, vote 13-0; Russia & China abstained). Gaza Peace Council membership (as of May 2026): 25 of 62 invited countries signed charter. India was invited but neither accepted nor declined (maintaining strategic autonomy on Middle East). Pakistan joined, seeking regional reconstruction leadership role. Board structure: Trump administration leadership, multilateral participation, private-sector reconstruction financing (estimated $100B+ needed). Board addresses: humanitarian access, hospital reconstruction, government formation, international oversight, reparations. Board represented Trump's pivot toward conflict resolution/reconstruction after military-first foreign policy emphasis, validating Davos 2026's 'Spirit of Dialogue' theme. India's selective engagement protected Indian interests (Iran partnership, Saudi relations) without full commitment to US-led reconstruction.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21091,"question":"UN General Assembly's 80th Session (UNGA 80) opened September 9, 2025. Who was elected President of UNGA 80, and what was the General Debate theme?","option_a":"Csaba Kőrösi (Hungary); 'Solutions through Solidarity'","option_b":"Annalena Baerbock (Germany); 'Better Together: 80 Years and More for Peace, Development, and Human Rights'","option_c":"Dennis Francis (Trinidad & Tobago); 'Rebuilding the UN'","option_d":"Philémon Yang (Cameroon); 'Leaving No One Behind'","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Annalena Baerbock (former German Foreign Minister 2022-2025, Green Party) elected President of UNGA 80. General Debate theme: 'Better Together: 80 Years and More for Peace, Development, and Human Rights' — emphasizing inclusive multilateralism and renewed commitment to UN Charter 80 years post-founding (1945). High-Level Week: September 22-30, 2025. UNGA 80 built on Pact for the Future (adopted Sept 2024, 56 specific actions addressing climate, development, peace, technology). PGA 79: Philémon Yang (Cameroon). India participated actively in UNGA 80, advancing climate justice, development financing, technology transfer agendas consistent with G20 2023 presidency priorities. Baerbock's selection reflected Germany's commitment to multilateral institutions and European diplomatic leadership.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21092,"question":"The 52nd G7 Summit 2026 will be held in Évian-les-Bains, France. What are its dates?","option_a":"May 10-12, 2026","option_b":"June 15-17, 2026","option_c":"July 20-22, 2026","option_d":"September 5-7, 2026","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"G7 Évian 2026: June 15-17, 2026 in Évian-les-Bains (Haute-Savoie, French Alps, near Switzerland/Geneva). Second time Évian hosts (previously 29th G8 summit, June 1-3, 2003 under Chirac). Host sequence: Italy Puglia (2024) → Canada Kananaskis (2025) → France Évian (2026). President Emmanuel Macron will host. Évian 2026 expected to address: European defense spending post-NATO expansion, US-Europe tariff tensions, Ukraine settlement prospects, China technology competition, AI governance. Macron's hosting underscores France's assertion of European autonomy from Trump's transactionalism. Location's proximity to Geneva/Switzerland symbolizes G7 reconnection with multilateral diplomacy after Kananaskis fragmentation. India observes G7 as barometer of Western unity/disarray and opportunity to advance plurilateral alternatives (BRICS, QUAD).","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21093,"question":"When was the African Union (AU) admitted as a permanent member of the G20, and at which summit?","option_a":"Bali Summit 2022 (Indonesia)","option_b":"New Delhi Summit 2023 (India, under PM Modi)","option_c":"Rio Summit 2024 (Brazil)","option_d":"Johannesburg Summit 2025 (South Africa)","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"African Union granted permanent G20 membership at the New Delhi Summit (September 9, 2023, under India's G20 Presidency). PM Modi welcomed AU in his opening remarks as historic affirmation of African representation in global economic governance. AU is the SECOND regional bloc to join G20 (after EU in 1997). Group sometimes informally called 'G21' post-AU admission (19 individual countries + EU + AU = 21 members). AU membership represented India's deliberate effort to elevate Africa's voice within G20, countering Western dominance. AU's permanent status provided platform for African nations to influence global development agenda, debt sustainability, climate finance, technology transfer discussions. AU membership validated emerging-economy driven G20 reform agenda and Global South coalition-building.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21094,"question":"G20 chairmanship was formally handed over from South Africa to the USA on which date in 2026?","option_a":"January 1, 2026","option_b":"January 12, 2026","option_c":"February 14, 2026","option_d":"March 1, 2026","correct_answer":"B","explanation":"South Africa formally handed over G20 chairmanship to the USA on January 12, 2026 — a low-key, offsite ceremony (not at main summit), reflecting US absence from Johannesburg Summit and departure from G20 ceremonial tradition. US G20 2026 will be held Miami (exact date TBD, tentatively November). Offsite handover represented diplomatic reality: US absence from predecessor summit made formal on-summit ceremony impractical. Ceremony pattern shows G20 flexibility: when host nations skip predecessor summit, handover ceremony moves to separate diplomatic event. South Africa's efficient Johannesburg Summit (declaration adopted at opening) and subsequent handover demonstrated multilateralism can proceed without hegemonic consensus — validating South Africa's diplomatic assertion and India's G20 2023 precedent emphasizing Global South outcomes.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21095,"question":"At G20 Johannesburg 2025, the 'Mission 300' initiative aims to achieve what by 2030?","option_a":"Connect 300 million Africans to electricity","option_b":"Plant 300 million trees in the Amazon","option_c":"Train 300 million youth in digital skills","option_d":"Mobilise $300 million for SIDS adaptation","correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Mission 300 (G20 Johannesburg 2025): connect 300 million Africans to electricity by 2030 — flagship energy access & development initiative led by World Bank + African Development Bank, embraced by South Africa's G20 presidency as symbol of African development priorities. Africa currently has world's lowest electrification rate (~600 million people without reliable electricity access, concentrated in Sub-Saharan Africa). Mission 300 addresses: energy poverty (undermining health, education, economic development), climate transition (renewable energy capacity building), productive employment (manufacturing, agriculture value-addition). Financing mechanism: concessional loans + grants from multilateral development banks. India supports Mission 300 as aligned with development-first G20 agenda and South-South cooperation. Initiative positions electrification as foundational development right and prerequisite for SDG achievement.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21096,"question":"India-UK CETA 2025 (signed July 24, 2025) is expected to come into force in which month, after UK parliamentary ratification?","option_a":"December 2025","option_b":"February 2026","option_c":"May 2026","option_d":"October 2026","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"India-UK CETA signed July 24, 2025; UK parliamentary ratification (House of Commons, House of Lords) expected completion by April 2026; entry into force: May 2026 (estimated). Ratification process: UK Trade Continuity Act (2022) provides expedited procedure for future trade agreements; minimum 21-day parliamentary scrutiny. CETA implementation: eliminates UK tariffs on 99% of Indian exports (textiles, pharma, engineering, seafood); reduces tariffs on 90% of UK exports (whisky, medical devices, aerospace, lamb). Projected bilateral trade increase: $34B/year from 2040. Trade impact timeline: immediate tariff elimination takes effect upon ratification, with some sensitive goods (spirits) phased over 10 years. India expects May 2026 ratification as validation of post-Brexit UK pivot toward Indo-Pacific partnerships.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21097,"question":"Which of the following is the CORRECT chair sequence for ASEAN from 2024 to 2027?","option_a":"Laos → Malaysia → Philippines → Singapore","option_b":"Indonesia → Laos → Malaysia → Philippines","option_c":"Vietnam → Laos → Thailand → Philippines","option_d":"Laos → Vietnam → Malaysia → Singapore","correct_answer":"A","explanation":"ASEAN Chair sequence: Laos (2024) → Malaysia (2025, 47th Summit KL) → Philippines (2026, 48th Summit Cebu) → Singapore (2027). ASEAN follows strict alphabetical rotation by English country names, ensuring all members eventually chair and preventing power imbalances. Each chair manages annual leadership, coordinates summits, sets regional priorities. ASEAN 11 members (post-Timor-Leste expansion Oct 2025): Brunei, Cambodia, Indonesia, Laos, Malaysia, Myanmar, Philippines, Singapore, Thailand, Vietnam, Timor-Leste. Next rotation cycle after Singapore 2027: Thailand (2028) → Vietnam (2029) → Brunei (2030, completing cycle) → Cambodia (2031). This predictable rotation builds institutional stability and shared responsibility across ASEAN family. India coordinates with chair to advance bilateral priorities and Act East Policy objectives.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21098,"question":"Match the 2026 summit to its host city: (i) G20 (ii) G7 (iii) APEC (iv) BRICS. Choose the CORRECT combination.","option_a":"(i) Miami (ii) Évian (iii) Shenzhen (iv) New Delhi","option_b":"(i) Washington DC (ii) Paris (iii) Beijing (iv) Mumbai","option_c":"(i) New York (ii) Lyon (iii) Shanghai (iv) Bangalore","option_d":"(i) Houston (ii) Évian (iii) Phu Quoc (iv) New Delhi","correct_answer":"A","explanation":"CORRECT 2026 host cities: (i) G20 → Miami, USA (date TBD, tentatively November); (ii) G7 → Évian-les-Bains, France (June 15-17); (iii) APEC → Shenzhen, China (Nov 18-19, announced Nov 1, 2025); (iv) BRICS → New Delhi, India (Sept 12-13, India Chair 2026). Other major 2026 multilateral fixtures: NATO → Ankara, Turkey (July 7-8); CHOGM → St. John's, Antigua & Barbuda (Nov 1-4); COP31 → Antalya, Turkey (November); ASEAN → Cebu, Philippines (already held May 6-8, 2026). This question tests comprehensive knowledge of 2026 multilateral calendar and geopolitical importance of summit locations. India's New Delhi BRICS chair positioning India as Global South leader; France's Évian hosting emphasizing European autonomy; China's Shenzhen underlining Asian economic centrality; USA's Miami suggesting Western economic focus.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21099,"question":"As of May 2026, which has been the status of the India-hosted Quad Leaders' Summit (originally due in India's 2025 chair year)?","option_a":"Held in November 2025 with all four leaders","option_b":"Officially cancelled and replaced by a foreign ministers' format permanently","option_c":"Not held in 2025; still not formally scheduled — Jaishankar in Feb 2026 said it was 'not cancelled' but unscheduled","option_d":"Shifted to Tokyo, Japan in March 2026","correct_answer":"C","explanation":"India's 2025 QUAD Chairmanship (USA 2024 → India 2025 → Australia 2026 → Japan 2027) concluded WITHOUT a formal leaders' summit — symbolic setback reflecting Trump's skepticism toward multilateral forums. EAM S. Jaishankar (February 15, 2026): QUAD summit was 'not cancelled' but 'unscheduled' (diplomatic language indicating lack of Trump administration enthusiasm). Trump's return signalled QUAD deprioritization compared to bilateral US-China engagement and skepticism toward alliance commitments. India adapted by maintaining operational QUAD delivery: MAITRI (maritime training operationalized June 2025), QUAD-at-Sea Ship Observer Mission, Critical Minerals Initiative (supply-chain resilience), Ports of the Future Partnership (Mumbai, Oct 2025), QUAD STEM scholarships (50 Indo-Pacific students). QUAD FM meeting (March 2026, New Delhi, Rubio attending) substituted for leaders' summit, framed as substantive political discussion. India's flexibility protected QUAD's institutional continuity despite external pressure.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
    {"id":21100,"question":"Which of the following statements about 2025-26 summit themes is INCORRECT?","option_a":"WEF Davos 2026 theme was 'A Spirit of Dialogue'","option_b":"G20 South Africa 2025 theme was 'Solidarity, Equality, Sustainability'","option_c":"APEC China 2026 theme is 'Building an Asia-Pacific Community to Prosper Together'","option_d":"ASEAN Philippines 2026 theme was 'Rebuilding Trust in Southeast Asia'","correct_answer":"D","explanation":"INCORRECT: ASEAN Philippines 2026 theme was 'Navigating Our Future, Together' (48th ASEAN Summit, Cebu, May 6-8, 2026) — NOT 'Rebuilding Trust in Southeast Asia' (that confused WEF Davos 2024 theme). CORRECT summit themes 2025-2026: WEF Davos 2025 = 'Collaboration for the Intelligent Age'; WEF Davos 2026 = 'A Spirit of Dialogue'; G20 South Africa 2025 = 'Solidarity, Equality, Sustainability'; APEC China 2026 = 'Building an Asia-Pacific Community to Prosper Together'; ASEAN Philippines 2026 = 'Navigating Our Future, Together'; CHOGM 2026 = 'Accelerating Partnerships and Investment for a Prosperous Commonwealth'; G7 France 2026 = (not specified, but likely European security-focused); NATO 2026 = (Ankara, Turkey, security-focused). This question tests detailed knowledge of multilateral summit messaging and thematic coherence across forums.","folder":"AP_HC","topic":"International_Current_Affairs"},
]


